window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-09-1", "source": "2022年09月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the  sentence “Nowadays more   and  more  students  are becoming increasingly  aware  of the importance of developing digital skills.” You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Nowadays more and more students are becoming increasingly aware of the importance of developing digital\nskills. This awareness is well founded, for in an age reshaped by technology, the ability to handle digital tools\nhas become as basic as reading and writing.\nThere are solid reasons behind this trend. For one thing, digital competence directly boosts academic\nefficiency. In my own case, learning to use spreadsheets and reference software turned a painful week of\nmanual data sorting into an afternoon's work. Moreover, such skills open doors in the job market. For instance,\na classmate who taught herself basic programming stood out among hundreds of applicants and landed an\ninternship at a tech firm. As a result, students who master these tools gain a clear edge both in study and\nin career.\nIn conclusion, the growing attention to digital skills reflects a genuine need rather than a passing fashion.\nStudents would be wise to invest time in mastering practical tools instead of waiting until graduation, so that\nthey can thrive in a world that runs on technology."}, "translation": {"prompt": "贴春联 (Spring Festival couplets) 是中国人欢度春节的一个重要习俗。春联由一对诗句和四字横批 (horizontal scroll) 组成，诗句和横批用金色或黑色写在红纸上，红色代表幸运， 金色代表财富。春联贴在大门左右两侧和门框上方。春联的诗句体现中国传统诗词的特点， 两句诗的字数相同、内容相关。横批凸显春联的主题，更是锦上添花。春联以简洁的文字描绘生动的形象，抒发美好的愿望。当家家户户贴春联时，人们就会意识到春节己经正式拉开序幕。", "sample": "Pasting Spring Festival couplets is an important custom for Chinese people during the Spring Festival. A Spring Festival couplet consists of a pair of poetic lines and a four-character horizontal scroll. Both the lines and the scroll are written in gold or black on red paper, with red symbolizing good luck and gold symbolizing wealth. The couplets are pasted on both sides of the door and above the doorframe. The poetic lines reflect the features of traditional Chinese poetry: the two lines have the same number of characters and are related in content. While the horizontal scroll highlights the theme of the couplet, it also adds the finishing touch. With concise words, Spring Festival couplets depict vivid images and express good wishes. Finally, when every household pastes Spring Festival couplets, people realize that the Spring Festival has officially begun."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2022-09-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The now extinct passenger pigeon has the dubious honor of being the last species anyone ever expected to disappear. At one point, there were more passenger pigeons than any other species of bird. Rough  __26__  of their population went as high as five billion and they accounted for around 40 percent of the total indigenous bird population of North America in the early 19th century.\nDespite their huge population, passenger pigeons were  __27__  to human intrusion into their nesting territory. Their nests were shabby things and two weeks after the eggs  __28__  , the parent pigeons would abandon their offspring, leaving them to take care of themselves. People discovered that these baby pigeons were really tasty, and the adult birds were also quite  __29__  . First the Native Americans and then the transplanted Europeans came to consider the birds a great  __30__  .\nBy the 1850s, commercial trapping of passenger pigeons was proceeding at an  __31__  pace. Hundreds of thousands of the birds were being harvested every day to be made into popular pigeon pies. In addition, large  __32__  of the pigeons' nesting territory were being cleared away for planting crops and creating pasture land. As numerous as the passenger pigeons were, they were not an  __33__  resource. By the 1880s, it was noticed that the bird population had become seriously  __34__  . The last passenger pigeons killed in the wild were shot in 1899.\nEventually those billions and billions of birds shrank to a single remaining  __35__  , a passenger pigeon named Martha, who died on September 1,1914, in captivity at the Cincinnati Zoo. In addition to being the end of an era, it was also the first time humans were able to exactly time the extinction of a species.", "matchIntro": "Is computer coding a foreign language?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "As computer coding has become an increasingly sought-after skill, more K-12 schools are working it into their curriculums. Some states have considered allowing students to forgo(放弃) foreign language for coding classes, despite opposition from educators."}, {"label": "B", "text": "There's a debate over whether it's appropriate to teach coding in elementary schools, with fierce opinions on each side. When it comes to allowing coding to fill foreign language requirements, though, most educators agree: Coding should be added to curriculums, but not at the expense of foreign language classes."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The idea is that computer programming is a language, allowing people to communicate with machines and programs. It's the language of the 21st century and more valuable than a natural language, some advocates argue. The computer science field is growing faster than schools can keep up because of budget constraints and a lack of skills training for teachers."}, {"label": "D", "text": "According to the 2016 U.S. News/Raytheon STEM Index, computer science jobs have helped boost wages in the U.S., and computer-related jobs hold the top seven positions in STEM fields for highest number of workers. Foreign language interest, on the other hand, is declining for the first time since 1995. The number of higher education language enrollments declined between 2009 and 2013 by more than 111,000 spots, according to the Modern Language Association of America."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"I think the opportunity to give people a choice is important,\" says Florida state Senator Jeremy Ring, who introduced a bill last year that would allow Florida students to choose between foreign language and coding classes for the purpose of university admissions requirements, \"I think if you're going to give two years of language in high school, you might as well do computer coding.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "The Florida bill died this year after passing 35:5 in the state Senate when the full Legislature failed to take action. It would have been the first state to try this initiative. Ring says that although he will be out of office, an identical bill will be reintroduced within the next year by others on his behalf. \"In the speech I gave on the Senate floor, I said, 'We can be the first state to do this, or we can be the 50th state to do it. It's our choice. It's going to happen,'\" Ring says."}, {"label": "G", "text": "A Kentucky bill similar to the one in Florida was met with complaints from educators, and was then amended to promote computer science education initiatives with no mention of foreign language requirements. Instead, the state will provide support for higher quality certified teachers for programming classes. Under the Washington bill, public universities would accept two years of computer science classes in place of two years of foreign language for admission purposes. A report detailing the opinions of state university officials is due to the Legislature by November 2017."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Texas passed a bill in 2013 that allows students to substitute computer coding only after they have attempted and performed poorly in a foreign language class. Srini Mandyam, CTO and co-founder of kid-friendly instructional coding company Tynker, believes allowing students to forgo foreign language because they struggle with it is unproductive because every subject, whether art, math or language, is a significant contribution to a well-rounded existence. \"Many students don't fare well with algebra but we never discuss eliminating it or … say chemistry is now counted as an algebra class,\" he said via email. \"We teach algebra because it's important and we should teach foreign language and coding for the same reason. Exposure to a wide breadth of subjects and material results in well-rounded students who are able to make informed decisions … about what they want to pursue.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "Computer science courses already fulfill a math or science high school graduation requirement in 28 states and the District of Columbia, up from only 12 states in 2013. And while advocates of the bills say they should count as foreign language instead, opponents stress the importance of balancing computer and foreign language skills."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Studies show that bilingualism(双语) correlates with cognitive development, intelligence, memory and problem solving abilities, according to the American Council on the Teaching of Foreign Languages. A 2007 study showed that foreign language students outperformed their non-foreign language peers on standardized tests after only two to three years of study. And while a 2014 report from German and American universities suggests that programmers are using language (but not mathematical) regions of the brain when understanding code, critics remain wary. They say that regardless of cognitive functions, being monolingual is a disadvantage in the increasingly international economy, even if English has become the de facto(事实上的) language of business."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"Our world is shrinking but its problems are really growing,\" says ACTFL National Language Teacher of the Year Ted Zarrow, who teaches high school Latin in Westwood, Massachusetts, and has also studied Spanish, French, German, Italian and Greek. \"We need to find a way to put ourselves at the global table and to treat each other with mutual respect. And learning languages allows us to do that because language is not part of culture, language is culture.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Even with the benefits and skill sets languages provide, recruiters and employers value computer skills more. According to the National Association of Colleges and Employers' 2016 report, study abroad and foreign language fluency were not very influential in the employee hiring processes, but 55 percent of employers looked for computer skills on applicants' resumes. However, although 2016 computer science graduates can expect to make the second highest starting salary compared with other jobs this year, the Bureau of Labor predicts the demand for computer programmers will decrease 8 percent or by 26,500 jobs by 2024."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Ring says foreign language skills are important, but expresses doubt that school districts could work both coding and language into their curriculum in a significant way because they lack the time in the school day. \"Nothing against language,\" he says. \"I just think it's something you have to start early and not just have something that you do for a couple of years in high school,\" he says."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Zarrow agrees that foreign language education should begin earlier, but says it is possible to work both computer programming and foreign language learning into schools evenly. He suggests an immersive, dual language program where students spend half the day in English and half the day in another language, as several schools around the country have successfully implemented. \"The study of language fosters a respect for diversity, a respect for ethnicity and really a respect for language,\" Zarrow says."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Though the benefits of computer programming skills are vast, foreign language and coding experts agree that computer science should be negotiated into curriculums rather than replacing foreign language outright. Mandyam says the two skill sets are essential but unrelated. \"Coding is an incredibly important 21st century skill for our kids to learn, and that's why we spend so much time trying to teach it,\" Mandyam says via email. \"But I believe it is the same as or even really comparable to learning a foreign language. It would be a shame to lose something so important for the sake of adding something else, even something as important as coding. Clearly, education leaders must figure out a way to teach both.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The U.S. and China don't agree on much these days. Germany and France share a border and a currency but are frequently at odds. The U.K. and India like to march to their own drum. But there's one issue on which all these countries see eye to eye: Technology companies are too big, too powerful, and too profitable. And that power is only likely to intensify, leaving governments with no choice but to confront it head-on by taking the companies to court, passing new competition laws, and perhaps even breaking up the tech giants.\nChina is the latest to implement an anti-trust crackdown, unveiling anti-monopoly rules last month. The draft rules followed the surprise suspension of a $37 billion stock offering by billionaire Jack Ma's Ant Group Co., making clear that no company can evade the government's regulation. The moves in China coincide with accelerating efforts in the U.S. and Europe to rein in Amazon.com, Apple, Facebook, and Google.\n\"The big get bigger and bigger but without being better,\" says Andreas Schwab, a German member of the European Parliament who championed a 2014 resolution to break up Google. \"Growing economic power, growing influence on local markets all over the world, and a growing concern of competitors and consumers altogether have made it happen now.\"\nIn this new anti-trust era, the old focus on pricing power no longer applies, because several of the biggest tech companies have established trillion-dollar monopolies by charging consumers next to nothing. Tech giants are increasingly assuming powerful positions in banking, finance, advertising, retail, and other markets that force smaller businesses to rely on their platforms to reach customers.\nFor years, Europe alone confronted the power of digital giants. Governments were alarmed that European companies were failing to match Silicon Valley's innovations or to stop Google and Facebook from vacuuming up personal data and, with that, advertising revenue. Led by Margrethe Vestager, the European Union's competition chief, countries have sought to police the market and encourage fair play.\nIn China the crackdown has been driven at least partly by fear that the homegrown tech industry is becoming too powerful. The country has long championed Alibaba and Tencent, but their massive accumulation of data on the Chinese citizenry is a growing concern for Beijing.\nIn the U.S., a new breed of anti-trust experts argues that consideration should be given to privacy, control over data, workers' rights, and the overall impact on smaller companies. And the public in general has grown increasingly skeptical of social media companies. More than 60% say the sector has a negative effect on the country, and almost half want more regulation for social media, according to a 2020 Pew Research Center study.", "47": "The U.S. and China don't agree on much these days. Germany and France share a border and a currency but are frequently at odds. The U.K. and India like to march to their own drum. But there's one issue on which all these countries see eye to eye: Technology companies are too big, too powerful, and too profitable. And that power is only likely to intensify, leaving governments with no choice but to confront it head-on by taking the companies to court, passing new competition laws, and perhaps even breaking up the tech giants.\nChina is the latest to implement an anti-trust crackdown, unveiling anti-monopoly rules last month. The draft rules followed the surprise suspension of a $37 billion stock offering by billionaire Jack Ma's Ant Group Co., making clear that no company can evade the government's regulation. The moves in China coincide with accelerating efforts in the U.S. and Europe to rein in Amazon.com, Apple, Facebook, and Google.\n\"The big get bigger and bigger but without being better,\" says Andreas Schwab, a German member of the European Parliament who championed a 2014 resolution to break up Google. \"Growing economic power, growing influence on local markets all over the world, and a growing concern of competitors and consumers altogether have made it happen now.\"\nIn this new anti-trust era, the old focus on pricing power no longer applies, because several of the biggest tech companies have established trillion-dollar monopolies by charging consumers next to nothing. Tech giants are increasingly assuming powerful positions in banking, finance, advertising, retail, and other markets that force smaller businesses to rely on their platforms to reach customers.\nFor years, Europe alone confronted the power of digital giants. Governments were alarmed that European companies were failing to match Silicon Valley's innovations or to stop Google and Facebook from vacuuming up personal data and, with that, advertising revenue. Led by Margrethe Vestager, the European Union's competition chief, countries have sought to police the market and encourage fair play.\nIn China the crackdown has been driven at least partly by fear that the homegrown tech industry is becoming too powerful. The country has long championed Alibaba and Tencent, but their massive accumulation of data on the Chinese citizenry is a growing concern for Beijing.\nIn the U.S., a new breed of anti-trust experts argues that consideration should be given to privacy, control over data, workers' rights, and the overall impact on smaller companies. And the public in general has grown increasingly skeptical of social media companies. More than 60% say the sector has a negative effect on the country, and almost half want more regulation for social media, according to a 2020 Pew Research Center study.", "48": "The U.S. and China don't agree on much these days. Germany and France share a border and a currency but are frequently at odds. The U.K. and India like to march to their own drum. But there's one issue on which all these countries see eye to eye: Technology companies are too big, too powerful, and too profitable. And that power is only likely to intensify, leaving governments with no choice but to confront it head-on by taking the companies to court, passing new competition laws, and perhaps even breaking up the tech giants.\nChina is the latest to implement an anti-trust crackdown, unveiling anti-monopoly rules last month. The draft rules followed the surprise suspension of a $37 billion stock offering by billionaire Jack Ma's Ant Group Co., making clear that no company can evade the government's regulation. The moves in China coincide with accelerating efforts in the U.S. and Europe to rein in Amazon.com, Apple, Facebook, and Google.\n\"The big get bigger and bigger but without being better,\" says Andreas Schwab, a German member of the European Parliament who championed a 2014 resolution to break up Google. \"Growing economic power, growing influence on local markets all over the world, and a growing concern of competitors and consumers altogether have made it happen now.\"\nIn this new anti-trust era, the old focus on pricing power no longer applies, because several of the biggest tech companies have established trillion-dollar monopolies by charging consumers next to nothing. Tech giants are increasingly assuming powerful positions in banking, finance, advertising, retail, and other markets that force smaller businesses to rely on their platforms to reach customers.\nFor years, Europe alone confronted the power of digital giants. Governments were alarmed that European companies were failing to match Silicon Valley's innovations or to stop Google and Facebook from vacuuming up personal data and, with that, advertising revenue. Led by Margrethe Vestager, the European Union's competition chief, countries have sought to police the market and encourage fair play.\nIn China the crackdown has been driven at least partly by fear that the homegrown tech industry is becoming too powerful. The country has long championed Alibaba and Tencent, but their massive accumulation of data on the Chinese citizenry is a growing concern for Beijing.\nIn the U.S., a new breed of anti-trust experts argues that consideration should be given to privacy, control over data, workers' rights, and the overall impact on smaller companies. And the public in general has grown increasingly skeptical of social media companies. More than 60% say the sector has a negative effect on the country, and almost half want more regulation for social media, according to a 2020 Pew Research Center study.", "49": "The U.S. and China don't agree on much these days. Germany and France share a border and a currency but are frequently at odds. The U.K. and India like to march to their own drum. But there's one issue on which all these countries see eye to eye: Technology companies are too big, too powerful, and too profitable. And that power is only likely to intensify, leaving governments with no choice but to confront it head-on by taking the companies to court, passing new competition laws, and perhaps even breaking up the tech giants.\nChina is the latest to implement an anti-trust crackdown, unveiling anti-monopoly rules last month. The draft rules followed the surprise suspension of a $37 billion stock offering by billionaire Jack Ma's Ant Group Co., making clear that no company can evade the government's regulation. The moves in China coincide with accelerating efforts in the U.S. and Europe to rein in Amazon.com, Apple, Facebook, and Google.\n\"The big get bigger and bigger but without being better,\" says Andreas Schwab, a German member of the European Parliament who championed a 2014 resolution to break up Google. \"Growing economic power, growing influence on local markets all over the world, and a growing concern of competitors and consumers altogether have made it happen now.\"\nIn this new anti-trust era, the old focus on pricing power no longer applies, because several of the biggest tech companies have established trillion-dollar monopolies by charging consumers next to nothing. Tech giants are increasingly assuming powerful positions in banking, finance, advertising, retail, and other markets that force smaller businesses to rely on their platforms to reach customers.\nFor years, Europe alone confronted the power of digital giants. Governments were alarmed that European companies were failing to match Silicon Valley's innovations or to stop Google and Facebook from vacuuming up personal data and, with that, advertising revenue. Led by Margrethe Vestager, the European Union's competition chief, countries have sought to police the market and encourage fair play.\nIn China the crackdown has been driven at least partly by fear that the homegrown tech industry is becoming too powerful. The country has long championed Alibaba and Tencent, but their massive accumulation of data on the Chinese citizenry is a growing concern for Beijing.\nIn the U.S., a new breed of anti-trust experts argues that consideration should be given to privacy, control over data, workers' rights, and the overall impact on smaller companies. And the public in general has grown increasingly skeptical of social media companies. More than 60% say the sector has a negative effect on the country, and almost half want more regulation for social media, according to a 2020 Pew Research Center study.", "50": "The U.S. and China don't agree on much these days. Germany and France share a border and a currency but are frequently at odds. The U.K. and India like to march to their own drum. But there's one issue on which all these countries see eye to eye: Technology companies are too big, too powerful, and too profitable. And that power is only likely to intensify, leaving governments with no choice but to confront it head-on by taking the companies to court, passing new competition laws, and perhaps even breaking up the tech giants.\nChina is the latest to implement an anti-trust crackdown, unveiling anti-monopoly rules last month. The draft rules followed the surprise suspension of a $37 billion stock offering by billionaire Jack Ma's Ant Group Co., making clear that no company can evade the government's regulation. The moves in China coincide with accelerating efforts in the U.S. and Europe to rein in Amazon.com, Apple, Facebook, and Google.\n\"The big get bigger and bigger but without being better,\" says Andreas Schwab, a German member of the European Parliament who championed a 2014 resolution to break up Google. \"Growing economic power, growing influence on local markets all over the world, and a growing concern of competitors and consumers altogether have made it happen now.\"\nIn this new anti-trust era, the old focus on pricing power no longer applies, because several of the biggest tech companies have established trillion-dollar monopolies by charging consumers next to nothing. Tech giants are increasingly assuming powerful positions in banking, finance, advertising, retail, and other markets that force smaller businesses to rely on their platforms to reach customers.\nFor years, Europe alone confronted the power of digital giants. Governments were alarmed that European companies were failing to match Silicon Valley's innovations or to stop Google and Facebook from vacuuming up personal data and, with that, advertising revenue. Led by Margrethe Vestager, the European Union's competition chief, countries have sought to police the market and encourage fair play.\nIn China the crackdown has been driven at least partly by fear that the homegrown tech industry is becoming too powerful. The country has long championed Alibaba and Tencent, but their massive accumulation of data on the Chinese citizenry is a growing concern for Beijing.\nIn the U.S., a new breed of anti-trust experts argues that consideration should be given to privacy, control over data, workers' rights, and the overall impact on smaller companies. And the public in general has grown increasingly skeptical of social media companies. More than 60% say the sector has a negative effect on the country, and almost half want more regulation for social media, according to a 2020 Pew Research Center study.", "51": "Born from the accessibility of mass air travel, modern international tourism has been popularized as \"holiday-making\" in regions that offer comparative advantages of sand, sun and sea. Travel is often portrayed as a tool for personal growth and tourism as an economic motor for destination countries and cities. There is a tendency to assume that tourism is good for everyone involved.\nToday the big bang of tourism drives over 1.2 billion tourists across international borders annually. Many popular places are literally being loved to death. Recent protests in ports of call like Venice and Barcelona against disturbances created by cruise ships show the unfortunate consequences of emphasizing quantity over quality in tourism.\nUncontrolled tourism development has become a primary driver of social and environmental disruption. Tourism studies provide much documentation of the many negative social impacts of tourism and resulting resentment that local populations direct toward visitors.\nAntagonism toward tourists typically develops in mature, heavily visited destinations. Protests in heavily visited destinations suggest that traditional tourism has overstayed its welcome.\nResidents often become frustrated when the benefits of tourism are not felt locally. Although it can generate foreign exchange, income and employment, there's no guarantee that multinational hotel chains will allocate these benefits equitably among local communities.\nOn the contrary, when people stay at large resorts or on cruise ships, they make most of their purchases there, leaving local communities little opportunity to benefit from tourist spending. These forms of tourism widen economic and political gaps between haves and have-nots at local destinations.\nIn recent decades, local residents in destination communities also have found themselves negotiating new cultural boundaries, class dynamics, service industry roles and lifestyle transformations. For example, data show that tourism activity corresponds to increased social problems as local residents adopt the behaviors of tourists.\nWhat does all this mean for the everyday traveler?\nFirst, all tourists should make every effort to honor their hosts and respect local conditions. This means being prepared to adapt to local customs and norms, rather than expecting local conditions to adapt to travelers.\nSecond, tourism is a market-based activity and works best when consumers reward better performers. In the information age, there's little excuse for travelers being uninformed about where their vacation money goes and who it enriches.\nInformed travelers also are better able to distinguish between multinational companies and local entrepreneurs whose businesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic benefits for local residents. Such businesses are in love with the destination and are therefore deserving of market reward. In the long run, being a responsible traveler means ensuring net positive impacts for local people and environments. With the information available at our fingertips, there has never been more opportunity to do so.", "52": "Born from the accessibility of mass air travel, modern international tourism has been popularized as \"holiday-making\" in regions that offer comparative advantages of sand, sun and sea. Travel is often portrayed as a tool for personal growth and tourism as an economic motor for destination countries and cities. There is a tendency to assume that tourism is good for everyone involved.\nToday the big bang of tourism drives over 1.2 billion tourists across international borders annually. Many popular places are literally being loved to death. Recent protests in ports of call like Venice and Barcelona against disturbances created by cruise ships show the unfortunate consequences of emphasizing quantity over quality in tourism.\nUncontrolled tourism development has become a primary driver of social and environmental disruption. Tourism studies provide much documentation of the many negative social impacts of tourism and resulting resentment that local populations direct toward visitors.\nAntagonism toward tourists typically develops in mature, heavily visited destinations. Protests in heavily visited destinations suggest that traditional tourism has overstayed its welcome.\nResidents often become frustrated when the benefits of tourism are not felt locally. Although it can generate foreign exchange, income and employment, there's no guarantee that multinational hotel chains will allocate these benefits equitably among local communities.\nOn the contrary, when people stay at large resorts or on cruise ships, they make most of their purchases there, leaving local communities little opportunity to benefit from tourist spending. These forms of tourism widen economic and political gaps between haves and have-nots at local destinations.\nIn recent decades, local residents in destination communities also have found themselves negotiating new cultural boundaries, class dynamics, service industry roles and lifestyle transformations. For example, data show that tourism activity corresponds to increased social problems as local residents adopt the behaviors of tourists.\nWhat does all this mean for the everyday traveler?\nFirst, all tourists should make every effort to honor their hosts and respect local conditions. This means being prepared to adapt to local customs and norms, rather than expecting local conditions to adapt to travelers.\nSecond, tourism is a market-based activity and works best when consumers reward better performers. In the information age, there's little excuse for travelers being uninformed about where their vacation money goes and who it enriches.\nInformed travelers also are better able to distinguish between multinational companies and local entrepreneurs whose businesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic benefits for local residents. Such businesses are in love with the destination and are therefore deserving of market reward. In the long run, being a responsible traveler means ensuring net positive impacts for local people and environments. With the information available at our fingertips, there has never been more opportunity to do so.", "53": "Born from the accessibility of mass air travel, modern international tourism has been popularized as \"holiday-making\" in regions that offer comparative advantages of sand, sun and sea. Travel is often portrayed as a tool for personal growth and tourism as an economic motor for destination countries and cities. There is a tendency to assume that tourism is good for everyone involved.\nToday the big bang of tourism drives over 1.2 billion tourists across international borders annually. Many popular places are literally being loved to death. Recent protests in ports of call like Venice and Barcelona against disturbances created by cruise ships show the unfortunate consequences of emphasizing quantity over quality in tourism.\nUncontrolled tourism development has become a primary driver of social and environmental disruption. Tourism studies provide much documentation of the many negative social impacts of tourism and resulting resentment that local populations direct toward visitors.\nAntagonism toward tourists typically develops in mature, heavily visited destinations. Protests in heavily visited destinations suggest that traditional tourism has overstayed its welcome.\nResidents often become frustrated when the benefits of tourism are not felt locally. Although it can generate foreign exchange, income and employment, there's no guarantee that multinational hotel chains will allocate these benefits equitably among local communities.\nOn the contrary, when people stay at large resorts or on cruise ships, they make most of their purchases there, leaving local communities little opportunity to benefit from tourist spending. These forms of tourism widen economic and political gaps between haves and have-nots at local destinations.\nIn recent decades, local residents in destination communities also have found themselves negotiating new cultural boundaries, class dynamics, service industry roles and lifestyle transformations. For example, data show that tourism activity corresponds to increased social problems as local residents adopt the behaviors of tourists.\nWhat does all this mean for the everyday traveler?\nFirst, all tourists should make every effort to honor their hosts and respect local conditions. This means being prepared to adapt to local customs and norms, rather than expecting local conditions to adapt to travelers.\nSecond, tourism is a market-based activity and works best when consumers reward better performers. In the information age, there's little excuse for travelers being uninformed about where their vacation money goes and who it enriches.\nInformed travelers also are better able to distinguish between multinational companies and local entrepreneurs whose businesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic benefits for local residents. Such businesses are in love with the destination and are therefore deserving of market reward. In the long run, being a responsible traveler means ensuring net positive impacts for local people and environments. With the information available at our fingertips, there has never been more opportunity to do so.", "54": "Born from the accessibility of mass air travel, modern international tourism has been popularized as \"holiday-making\" in regions that offer comparative advantages of sand, sun and sea. Travel is often portrayed as a tool for personal growth and tourism as an economic motor for destination countries and cities. There is a tendency to assume that tourism is good for everyone involved.\nToday the big bang of tourism drives over 1.2 billion tourists across international borders annually. Many popular places are literally being loved to death. Recent protests in ports of call like Venice and Barcelona against disturbances created by cruise ships show the unfortunate consequences of emphasizing quantity over quality in tourism.\nUncontrolled tourism development has become a primary driver of social and environmental disruption. Tourism studies provide much documentation of the many negative social impacts of tourism and resulting resentment that local populations direct toward visitors.\nAntagonism toward tourists typically develops in mature, heavily visited destinations. Protests in heavily visited destinations suggest that traditional tourism has overstayed its welcome.\nResidents often become frustrated when the benefits of tourism are not felt locally. Although it can generate foreign exchange, income and employment, there's no guarantee that multinational hotel chains will allocate these benefits equitably among local communities.\nOn the contrary, when people stay at large resorts or on cruise ships, they make most of their purchases there, leaving local communities little opportunity to benefit from tourist spending. These forms of tourism widen economic and political gaps between haves and have-nots at local destinations.\nIn recent decades, local residents in destination communities also have found themselves negotiating new cultural boundaries, class dynamics, service industry roles and lifestyle transformations. For example, data show that tourism activity corresponds to increased social problems as local residents adopt the behaviors of tourists.\nWhat does all this mean for the everyday traveler?\nFirst, all tourists should make every effort to honor their hosts and respect local conditions. This means being prepared to adapt to local customs and norms, rather than expecting local conditions to adapt to travelers.\nSecond, tourism is a market-based activity and works best when consumers reward better performers. In the information age, there's little excuse for travelers being uninformed about where their vacation money goes and who it enriches.\nInformed travelers also are better able to distinguish between multinational companies and local entrepreneurs whose businesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic benefits for local residents. Such businesses are in love with the destination and are therefore deserving of market reward. In the long run, being a responsible traveler means ensuring net positive impacts for local people and environments. With the information available at our fingertips, there has never been more opportunity to do so.", "55": "Born from the accessibility of mass air travel, modern international tourism has been popularized as \"holiday-making\" in regions that offer comparative advantages of sand, sun and sea. Travel is often portrayed as a tool for personal growth and tourism as an economic motor for destination countries and cities. There is a tendency to assume that tourism is good for everyone involved.\nToday the big bang of tourism drives over 1.2 billion tourists across international borders annually. Many popular places are literally being loved to death. Recent protests in ports of call like Venice and Barcelona against disturbances created by cruise ships show the unfortunate consequences of emphasizing quantity over quality in tourism.\nUncontrolled tourism development has become a primary driver of social and environmental disruption. Tourism studies provide much documentation of the many negative social impacts of tourism and resulting resentment that local populations direct toward visitors.\nAntagonism toward tourists typically develops in mature, heavily visited destinations. Protests in heavily visited destinations suggest that traditional tourism has overstayed its welcome.\nResidents often become frustrated when the benefits of tourism are not felt locally. Although it can generate foreign exchange, income and employment, there's no guarantee that multinational hotel chains will allocate these benefits equitably among local communities.\nOn the contrary, when people stay at large resorts or on cruise ships, they make most of their purchases there, leaving local communities little opportunity to benefit from tourist spending. These forms of tourism widen economic and political gaps between haves and have-nots at local destinations.\nIn recent decades, local residents in destination communities also have found themselves negotiating new cultural boundaries, class dynamics, service industry roles and lifestyle transformations. For example, data show that tourism activity corresponds to increased social problems as local residents adopt the behaviors of tourists.\nWhat does all this mean for the everyday traveler?\nFirst, all tourists should make every effort to honor their hosts and respect local conditions. This means being prepared to adapt to local customs and norms, rather than expecting local conditions to adapt to travelers.\nSecond, tourism is a market-based activity and works best when consumers reward better performers. In the information age, there's little excuse for travelers being uninformed about where their vacation money goes and who it enriches.\nInformed travelers also are better able to distinguish between multinational companies and local entrepreneurs whose businesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic benefits for local residents. Such businesses are in love with the destination and are therefore deserving of market reward. In the long run, being a responsible traveler means ensuring net positive impacts for local people and environments. With the information available at our fingertips, there has never been more opportunity to do so."}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-09-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the man say about the weekly program?", "options": ["A) It enables people to earn more money.", "B) It teaches the importance of financing.", "C) It helps people with budgeting.", "D) It introduces a novel way to invest."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It helps people with budgeting.\n【定位】Welcome to Money Matters, a weekly program that helps you manage your money. Tonight,\nI'll be talking to Mary Johnson about budgeting.\n欢迎收听《金钱要事》，这是一档帮你管理钱财的每周节目。今晚，我将和玛丽·约翰逊聊聊\n预算的话题。\n【信号】开场点题句。节目开头的自我介绍 a weekly program that helps you manage your\nmoney 加上 talking to Mary Johnson about budgeting，是典型的\"开场点题\"信号——\n节目类录音常在第一两句报出自己是干嘛的，这句就是定位锚。\n【替换】talking to Mary Johnson about budgeting ↔ helps people with budgeting\n原词budgeting+功能补全\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音说节目\"helps you manage your money\"（帮你管钱），不是帮你\"earn more\nmoney\"（赚更多钱）；管钱 ≠ 赚钱，方向被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：helps you manage your money\nB) 未提及\n录音谈的是 budgeting（预算/记账），并没说节目\"teaches the importance of\nfinancing\"（讲融资/理财的重要性）；financing 与 budgeting 是相近理财词，盗用相近\n概念来诱选，原文未提\"重要性\"这一说法。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 introduces a novel way to invest 借\"理财\"氛围里的 invest 来诱选，但录音全程讲预\n算与记账，从未介绍\"新的投资方式\"，是听到理财就联想投资的陷阱。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the man read recently?", "options": ["A) Many Americans are not satisfied with their income.", "B) Many Americans have no idea about how to invest.", "C) Most Americans do not know how to save money.", "D) Most Americans do not stick to a budget."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Most Americans do not stick to a budget.\n【定位】That's easier said than done. I read recently that only 41% of Americans adhere to a budget.\n说起来容易做起来难。我最近读到，只有 41% 的美国人会坚持执行预算。\n【信号】I read recently 引出句。I read recently that only 41% of Americans adhere to a\nbudget 由\"I read recently that…\"直接引出\"读到的内容\"，是答案句的明确信号——\"读到\n了什么\"的答案就跟在该信号之后。\n【替换】only 41% of Americans adhere to a budget ↔ Most Americans do not stick to a budget.\n肯否转换+量词换算\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音这处只说\"坚持预算的比例低\"，没提美国人\"not satisfied with their income\"（对收入\n不满）；满意度是另起的话题，原文未涉及。\nB) 原词陷阱\nno idea about how to invest 借理财氛围里的 invest 诱选，但这句数据讲的是\"坚持预\n算\"，与\"会不会投资\"无关，是听到理财词就联想的陷阱。\nC) 偷换概念\ndo not know how to save money 把原文的\"坚持预算（budget）\"偷换成\"不会存钱\n（save）\"；后文虽另有 most people have no idea how much they spend，但那是\"不\n清楚花了多少\"，不等于\"不会存钱\"，且本题要的是\"读到的那条\"，对应的是 41% 那句。\n借⽤原⽂：adhere to a budget", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman suggest the man do first to avoid getting into debt?", "options": ["A) Keep track of his money.", "B) Live within his means.", "C) Find more sources of income.", "D) Refrain from buying luxuries."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Keep track of his money.\n【定位】Well, it's all about keeping track of your money. If you don't do that, you'll never be able to\nset any goals for your budget or have the discipline to stick to them.\n嗯，关键全在于记录你的钱。如果你不这么做，就永远没法为预算设定目标，也没有自律去\n坚持它们。\n【信号】it's all about 强调首要。it's all about keeping track of your money 用\"it's all\nabout…\"强调\"一切的根本在于\"，是把某动作点为\"首要/第一步\"的信号；后接 If you don't\ndo that… 进一步坐实它是前提，故这是\"先做什么\"的答案句。\n【替换】keeping track of your money ↔ Keep track of his money.\nnear原词+人称转换\n【排除】B) 答非所问\nLive within his means. 对应原文 If the result is positive, you are living within your\nmeans.，那是\"记账并做收支相减之后\"才谈到的结果，不是题目要的\"first（第一步）\"；盗\n用 living within your means 原词，却答非\"先做什么\"。\n借⽤原⽂：living within your means\nC) 原词陷阱\nFind more sources of income. 沾原文 adding up all of your sources of income 与 find\nyourself a part-time job 的边，但\"找更多收入来源\"在录音里是 Failing that（实在不行）\n的最后一招，绝非\"第一步\"。\n借⽤原⽂：sources of income\nD) 答非所问\nRefrain from buying luxuries. 对应原文 cutting back on those non-essential items，\n属\"算出是负数后\"要做的调整，同样不是\"第一步\"；它答非题目限定的 first。\n借⽤原⽂：cutting back on those non-essential items", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the woman say about online shopping?", "options": ["A) It offers a greater variety of items.", "B) It helps avoid unnecessary spending.", "C) It changes one's way of living.", "D) It saves one's time for shopping."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It helps avoid unnecessary spending.\n【定位】Try cutting back on those non-essential items, sell some stuff in your attic, or shop online to\navoid unnecessary temptations like chocolate.\n试着削减那些非必需品，把阁楼里的一些东西卖掉，或者网上购物以避开像巧克力那样不必\n要的诱惑。\n【信号】shop online to avoid 目的状语。shop online to avoid unnecessary temptations 里 to\navoid 是\"网购为了避开…\"的目的状语，直接点出网购的好处所在，是本题答案信号——好\n处就藏在 to 后面那一段。\n【替换】shop online to avoid unnecessary temptations like chocolate ↔\nIt helps avoid unnecessary spending.\n诱惑→花销的概括等值\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音只说网购能\"避开诱惑\"，没说它 offers a greater variety of items（商品更多\n样）；\"商品多\"是网购的生活常识，但本句原文没这么说。\nC) 过度推断\nIt changes one's way of living. 把\"避开一次诱惑\"夸成\"改变生活方式\"，原文 shop online\nto avoid unnecessary temptations 远没到\"改变生活方式\"的程度，是过度拔高。\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt saves one's time for shopping. 借\"shopping\"原词与网购\"省时\"的常识诱选，但录音说\n的是\"避开诱惑\"，从未提网购\"省时间\"，是听到 shop/online 就联想常识的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：shop online", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman say about the flat?", "options": ["A) It is brand-new.", "B) It has plenty of rooms.", "C) It belongs to her mother.", "D) It has been vacant for months."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It belongs to her mother.\n【定位】The place actually belongs to my mother, but her health isn't great, so we finally managed to\npersuade her to move in with us and rent this old place out.\n这地方其实是我母亲的，但她身体不太好，所以我们好不容易才说服她搬来跟我们一起住，\n把这所老房子租出去。\n【信号】女方亲口陈述归属，是直接应答型答案句。答案落在女方介绍房子时的第一句陈述 The\nplace actually belongs to my mother，\"actually\"带出的正是她要交代的房子归属事实，\n属\"被介绍方亲口给出\"的直接信号。\n【替换】The place actually belongs to my mother ↔ It belongs to her mother\n几乎原句对位，仅 The place→It、my→her\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt is brand-new借走了录音原词 brand-new，可那是 all the appliances are brand-new\n——全新的是电器，主语是 appliances，不是房子本身；听到 brand-new 就选房子全新，\n正中圈套。\n借⽤原⽂：all the appliances are brand-new\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt has plenty of rooms近似录音里的 Plenty of space，但那是男方 M 看房后的评价，且\n说的是\"空间大\"，并非女方\"对房子的陈述\"；题干问的是 woman，张冠李戴又偷换了\nspace→rooms。\n借⽤原⽂：Plenty of space\nD) 未提及\nIt has been vacant for months听上去合理，但全篇没有任何\"空置/数月\"的信息，女方只\n说服母亲搬走后\"租出去\"，并未提空置时长，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What is the man's chief consideration in looking for a flat?", "options": ["A) Space.", "B) Tranquillity.", "C) Appliances.", "D) Location."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Tranquillity.\n【定位】Now, the big question: What about noise and the neighbours?\n现在，最大的问题是：噪音和邻居怎么样？\n【信号】男方自报\"the big question\"，强调信号锁定焦点。答案句靠强调信号定位：男方用 the\nbig question亲手把\"噪音和邻居\"标成头等大事，紧接第6段女方回答 peaceful pond、\ntranquility 与之呼应，焦点正是\"安静\"。\n【替换】What about noise and the neighbours? ↔ Tranquillity\n\"不要噪音\"概括为\"安静\"，否定转肯定\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nSpace借走 Plenty of space，男方确实夸了空间大，但那是顺口称赞，并没被他标成\"the\nbig question\"，不是\"chief\"考虑；听到 space 就选，落入只听到原词不分主次的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：Plenty of space\nC) 原词陷阱\nAppliances照搬 all the appliances are brand-new的原词，可那是女方主动介绍的卖点，\n题干问的是 man's chief consideration，男方并未把电器当首要；既张冠李戴又\n非\"chief\"。\n借⽤原⽂：all the appliances are brand-new\nD) 过度推断\nLocation能从 Getting to work was a nightmare联想到位置/通勤，但那是男方吐槽现住\n处之苦的背景，并非他看这套房时拎出的\"the big question\"；把背景信息升格成\"首要考\n虑\"属过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：Getting to work was a nightmare", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man have to do on signing the contract?", "options": ["A) Talk to his wife about the contract terms.", "B) Pay the first month's rent and a deposit.", "C) Check the references of the flat owner.", "D) Consult his solicitor one more time."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Pay the first month's rent and a deposit.\n【定位】The first month's rent and a deposit are mandatory on signing the contract.\n签合同时，必须缴纳首月租金和一笔押金。\n【信号】mandatory 标出强制动作，触发条件 on signing 对齐。答案句靠强制信号定位：are\nmandatory on signing the contract中 mandatory（必须的）直接点出强制要求，且 on\nsigning the contract 与题干触发条件逐字对齐，落点无疑。\n【替换】The first month's rent and a deposit are mandatory ↔ Pay the first month's rent and a deposit\n名词\"是必须的\"改写成动词\"去支付\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTalk to his wife about the contract terms借了 contract 一词，可录音里男方提妻子是说\n她上班用车、鞋多，从没说签约时要\"和妻子商量合同条款\"；听到 contract 就选，正中原\n词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：as soon as I get a contract drawn up\nC) 张冠李戴\nCheck the references of the flat owner盗用 references，但原文是女方说 I spoke to all\nyour references and they all checked out okay——查证明人的是房东、查的是租客；选\n项把施受方整个调反，且与\"签约时\"无关。\n借⽤原⽂：I spoke to all your references and they all checked out okay\nD) 张冠李戴\nConsult his solicitor one more time借走 solicitor，可原文是女方说 as soon as I get a\ncontract drawn up with the solicitor——找律师拟合同的是女方；\"男方再咨询一次律\n师\"原文并无，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：as soon as I get a contract drawn up with the solicitor", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why does the man say his wife will feel very excited if they move into the flat?", "options": ["A) She can have a wonderful view of the pond.", "B) She will be much closer to her work place.", "C) She can make friends with new neighbours.", "D) She will have plenty of space for her shoes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） She will have plenty of space for her shoes.\n【定位】My wife will be thrilled to get out of the shabby place we are now in, and start filling those\nwardrobes with all those shoes.\n我妻子一定会兴奋极了，能搬出我们现在这破地方，开始把那些衣柜塞满她所有的鞋子。\n【信号】wife 被点名 + thrilled，兴奋理由紧贴其后。答案句靠\"本人被点名 + 情绪词\"定位：My\nwife will be thrilled里 thrilled 正对题干 very excited，其后紧跟的 filling those\nwardrobes with all those shoes就是兴奋的具体理由——衣柜能装下她所有鞋。\n【替换】filling those wardrobes with all those shoes ↔ She will have plenty of space for her shoes\n\"衣柜塞满鞋\"概括为\"有足够地方放鞋\"\nLots of closet space too, which is fabulous. My wife has a ridiculous number of shoes ↔\nShe will have plenty of space for her shoes\n前文 closet space + 鞋多，提前埋下\"放鞋空间\"伏笔\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nShe can have a wonderful view of the pond借走原词 pond，录音确有 overlooks a\nclear and peaceful pond，但那是女方介绍房况，男方说妻子兴奋时绑的是鞋、不是池\n塘；听到 pond 就选，正中原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：overlooks a clear and peaceful pond\nB) 过度推断\nShe will be much closer to her work place能从 she works nights at the hospital、上班\n难联想，但原文只说现住处通勤是噩梦，并没说搬进新房\"离单位更近\"或妻子为此兴奋，属\n过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：she works nights at the hospital\nC) 未提及\nShe can make friends with new neighbours借了 neighbours，可原文 all the\nneighbours are elderly只说邻居都上了年纪、不吵，从没说妻子会\"和新邻居交朋友\"，纯\n属添加。\n借⽤原⽂：all the neighbours are elderly", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What has the new study found about children watching television?", "options": ["A) The more commercials they see, the more brands they get to know.", "B) The more commercials they see, the more they adore celebrities.", "C) The more television they watch, the fewer conflicts in the family.", "D) The more television they watch, the greater their parents' stress."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The more television they watch, the greater their parents' stress.\n【定位】A new study has found a positive correlation between how much television children watch\nand their parents' stress levels.\n一项新研究发现，孩子看电视的多少与其家长的压力水平之间存在正相关。\n【信号】开头点题句即研究结论。篇章独白常在第一句就抛出研究核心，本段开头 A new study has\nfound a positive correlation 用 \"研究发现\" 直接报出结论，是典型的开头点题信号，听到\n\"A new study has found\" 就该锁定这句为答案句。\n【替换】a positive correlation between how much television children watch and their parents' stress levels ↔\nThe more television they watch, the greater their parents' stress\n正相关改写成 \"越多越大\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确说看电视越多 the more they're exposed to advertising，但谈的是接触广告进而\n吵架、增压，并非 \"认识更多品牌\"；A 借 commercials 这个真出现过的话题词，把结果偷\n换成认识品牌，是听到原词就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：the more they're exposed to advertising\nB) 张冠李戴\n明星 celebrity endorsements 确在录音里出现，但那是第 4 段讲广告手段时说的，与本题\n问的 \"研究发现\" 无关；B 把后文一个原词搬来当本题结论，属张冠李戴式的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：celebrity endorsements\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说看电视多会 generate conflict（产生冲突、增加压力），C 却说 \"家庭冲突更少\"，\n把录音的因果方向整个反了过来，属与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：This could generate conflict if the parents refuse", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What are parents advised to do to reduce the impact of TV commercials?", "options": ["A) Inform children of the family's financial situation.", "B) Shift children's attention to interesting activities.", "C) Involve children in making purchasing decisions.", "D) Help children understand advertising's intent."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Involve children in making purchasing decisions.\n【定位】The researchers suggest that parents seek input from their children on family\npurchasing decisions.\n研究者建议，家长在家庭购买决策上征求孩子的意见。\n【信号】建议词 suggest 领出做法。本句以 The researchers suggest that parents… 开头，\nsuggest 是明确的建议信号词，它后面带出的那个做法就是 \"家长被建议做什么\" 的答案，\n听到 suggest 即锁定此句。\n【替换】parents seek input from their children on family purchasing decisions ↔\nInvolve children in making purchasing decisions\n征求意见=让孩子参与决策\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从未让家长告知孩子家庭财务状况，financial situation 在原文找不到对应，属凭常识\n臆想的未提及项。\nB) 未提及\n录音提到的应对是关掉电视和改变沟通方式，从未说 \"把孩子注意力转移到有趣活动上\"；\ninteresting activities 是凭空添加，属未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nadvertising's intent 确在录音出现，但它是第 4 段解释 \"孩子为何易受影响\" 时说孩子缺乏\n理解广告意图的认知能力，并非给家长的建议；D 借这个真原词冒充本题答案，是原词陷\n阱。\n借⽤原⽂：children don't have the cognitive ability to fully understand advertising's intent", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What makes children particularly vulnerable to TV commercials?", "options": ["A) Their limited cognitive ability.", "B) Their strong natural curiosity.", "C) Their admiration for celebrities.", "D) Their lack of social experience."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Their limited cognitive ability.\n【定位】Plus, children don't have the cognitive ability to fully understand advertising's intent. That\nmakes them particularly vulnerable to advertisements.\n再加上，孩子不具备充分理解广告意图的认知能力。这使他们特别容易受广告影响。\n【信号】That makes them… 收束因果。末句 That makes them particularly vulnerable 用\n\"That makes them…\" 把前一句的原因（缺乏认知能力）与 \"特别易受影响\" 的结果直接挂\n钩，是明确的因果收束信号，听到它就回指前句去找原因。\n【替换】children don't have the cognitive ability to fully understand advertising's intent ↔\nTheir limited cognitive ability\n\"没有充分认知能力\"=\"认知能力有限\"\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音从未说孩子因 \"天生好奇\" 而易受影响，natural curiosity 在原文无对应，是凭常识臆\n测的未提及项。\nC) 原词陷阱\ncelebrity endorsements 确在录音里出现，但它是广告吸引孩子的手段之一，不是 \"孩子易\n受影响\" 的原因；C 借 celebrities 这个真原词，把广告的手段偷换成孩子的特质，是原词陷\n阱。\n借⽤原⽂：celebrity endorsements\nD) 未提及\n录音解释原因时只说孩子缺乏认知能力，从未提 \"缺乏社会经验\"，social experience 在原\n文找不到对应，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What baffling phenomenon is discussed in the passage?", "options": ["A) Bad guys may do good deeds.", "B) Bad guys may go unpunished.", "C) Good guys may not always cooperate.", "D) Good guys may get unfair treatment."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Good guys may get unfair treatment.\n【定位】But sometimes, the good guys also get punished or criticized, specifically because they are\nso good.\n但有时候，好人也会仅仅因为他们太好，而恰恰因此受到惩罚或批评。\n【信号】转折 But 后才是真意。前文先说 we do indeed reward good people（确实奖励好人）做\n铺垫，紧接 But sometimes, the good guys also get punished or criticized 用 But 一\n转，转折之后才是作者真正要立的反常现象——这句就是答案句。\n【替换】the good guys also get punished or criticized ↔ Good guys may get unfair treatment\n被罚被批=不公待遇\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项把录音里的 bad guys 搬来拼成「坏人也会做好事」，但录音从没说坏人做好事，只说\nwe should only punish the bad guys；听到 bad guys 就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：we should only punish the bad guys\nB) 与原文相反\n录音说坏人「会被」惩罚（often punish people who harm others），选项却说坏人「逃\n脱」惩罚（go unpunished），把极性反了；盗用 punish 一词却改了方向。\n借⽤原⽂：We also often punish people who harm others\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项说「好人不总愿合作」，盗用了 aren't good team players 里的合作意味，但录音说\n的是不合作的人会被罚，没说好人不合作；这是把别处的合作话题安到「好人」头上。\n借⽤原⽂：who aren't good team players", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "How are we all judged according to the passage?", "options": ["A) By what we do.", "B) By fair evaluation.", "C) In comparison with others.", "D) In accordance with set standards."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） In comparison with others.\n【定位】After all, we're all judged in comparison with others.\n毕竟，我们所有人都是在与他人的比较中被评判的。\n【信号】judged 原词直接应答。题干问 how are we judged，录音 we're all judged in\ncomparison with others 里 judged 一词与题干正面咬合，是直接给出依据的应答句；句\n首 After all（毕竟）是收束总结语气，更确认这是作者下的判定。\n【替换】in comparison with others ↔ In comparison with others\n近乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项「凭我们做什么」看似呼应录音里 stopping that person from looking good 的行为\n话题，但题干问的是评判依据，录音明说依据是「比较」不是「我们做的事」；用行为话题\n误导。\n借⽤原⽂：incentive in stopping that person from looking good\nB) 未提及\n选项「靠公正评价」里的 fair（公正）录音通篇没出现，是凭「评判」话题臆造出的好听说\n法；录音说的是与人比较，并未强调公正。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项「按固定标准」恰与录音「与他人比较」相对立——录音明确说是相对的比较，不是绝\n对的标准；这是给出与答案句直接相反的另一极。\n借⽤原⽂：we're all judged in comparison with others", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What can a normal person do when faced with people who perform better?", "options": ["A) Learn from them earnestly.", "B) Compete with them actively.", "C) Leave them alone temporarily.", "D) Cooperate with them sincerely."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Compete with them actively.\n【定位】When faced with someone better, what can a normal person do? One option is to\nactively compete.\n面对比自己更强的人，一个普通人能做什么呢？一个选择是积极地去竞争。\n【信号】设问后第一答 One option。录音用 what can a normal person do? 自设问，紧接 One\noption is to actively compete 给出第一个、也是唯一正面的做法；One option 是列举做\n法的起始信号，作者把「主动竞争」摆在首位，正是答案句。\n【替换】One option is to actively compete ↔ Compete with them actively\ncompete/actively 同义重组\n【排除】A) 未提及\n「向他们认真学习」是面对强者最符合常识的反应，但录音列举的做法里只有竞争和打压，\n没有 learn；这是用常识替文本，录音根本没念。\nC) 未提及\n「暂时不理他们」录音从未提及，作者给的第二选择是 bring that person down（打\n压），不是回避；这是凭想象补的做法。\n借⽤原⽂：A second option is to bring that person down\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项「真诚地与他们合作」盗用了录音里 suppress their cooperation 的 cooperation 一\n词，但录音说的是「压制别人的合作」，方向恰恰相反，绝非自己去真诚合作。\n借⽤原⽂：suppress their cooperation or work ethic", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What may discourage people from performing good deeds?", "options": ["A) Being dismissed as hypocritical.", "B) Not getting the reward they deserve.", "C) Having to make too many sacrifices.", "D) Being misunderstood by people around."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Being dismissed as hypocritical.\n【定位】Such good deeds are dismissed as naive or hypocritical by those who do not perform those\ndeeds. This criticism may ultimately discourage people from doing good deeds.\n这样的善行被那些自己不做这些事的人贬斥为天真或虚伪。这种批评最终可能打消人们行善\n的念头。\n【信号】discourage 前一句即诱因。题干的 discourage 在 This criticism may ultimately\ndiscourage people from doing good deeds 原词复现，而 This criticism 回指上一句\ndismissed as naive or hypocritical；指代回指处的那项批评（被斥为伪善）就是打击行善\n的因素，是答案句。\n【替换】dismissed as naive or hypocritical ↔ Being dismissed as hypocritical\n近义并保留 hypocritical\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n「得不到应有回报」呼应录音前文 reward good people 的奖励话题，但那是开头铺垫，并\n非第 4 段 discourage 的诱因；把别段的内容安到本题头上。\n借⽤原⽂：we do indeed reward good people\nC) 未提及\n「不得不做太多牺牲」录音通篇没提 sacrifice，是对行善代价的常识联想；录音说的打击是\n被斥伪善，不是牺牲过多。\nD) 偷换概念\n「被周围人误解」听着像 dismissed as hypocritical，但「误解」是中性的认知偏差，录音\n说的是被恶意贬斥为伪善，程度与性质都不同，属把「被斥伪善」悄悄换成更轻的「被误\n解」。\n借⽤原⽂：dismissed as naive or hypocritical by those who do not perform those deeds", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about most preteen children?", "options": ["A) They are increasingly aware of gender differences.", "B) They engage themselves in positive recollection.", "C) They begin to see the importance of friendship.", "D) They make friends with peers of the same sex."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They make friends with peers of the same sex.\n【定位】Most preteen children have a best friend who is usually someone of the same sex and\nsimilar age.\n大多数青春期前的孩子都有一个最好的朋友，这个朋友通常是同性、年龄相仿的。\n【信号】总括词most + 段首点题。这句出现在第二段段首，且以题干同款的 Most preteen\nchildren 开头，是讲者对该群体的总括陈述；听到 most 这种总括词紧跟题目所问对象，就\n该锁定其后内容为答案句。\n【替换】a best friend who is usually someone of the same sex ↔ make friends with peers of the same sex\nhave a best friend概括为make friends\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ngender differences 借用了第一段反复出现的 gender differences 一词，但那是讲者引出\n全文话题的背景，并非对 preteen children 的描述；说 preteen 孩子『越来越意识到性别\n差异』在该年龄段段落里并无对应。\n借⽤原⽂：gender differences are very much in the media these days\nB) 原词陷阱\npositive recollection 与录音 positive recollection of these childhood friendships 几乎\n原词照搬，极具迷惑性；但原文说的是『两性都对这些童年友谊有正面回忆』，主体是回忆\n童年的成年视角，并非在讲 preteen 孩子当下『正在进行正面回忆』这一行为，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Both sexes share an essentially positive recollection of these childhood friendships\nC) 未提及\nsee the importance of friendship 听上去合情合理，但录音在描述 preteen children 时只\n说他们有同性好友、玩不同性质的游戏，从未说他们『开始认识到友谊的重要性』，属于无\n中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do most males devote much of their energy to during adolescence?", "options": ["A) Competing for position.", "B) Forging close ties with friends.", "C) Pursuing fashion.", "D) Fulfilling family obligations."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Competing for position.\n【定位】Now, however, with heightened intensity, considerable energy is devoted to competing for\nposition and a definite undercurrent of competition permeates the relationship.\n然而此时，随着强度增加，大量精力被投入到争夺地位上，一股明确的竞争暗流贯穿于这段\n关系之中。\n【信号】转折however后 + energy is devoted to原词。本句以转折词 however 引出青春期男性\n的真实状态，并直接出现题干同款短语 considerable energy is devoted to competing\nfor position；转折之后才是讲者要强调的结论，加上 devoted to 这一投入信号，定位即\n落于此。\n【替换】considerable energy is devoted to competing for position ↔ Competing for position\n几乎原词对应、仅句式调整\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nForging close ties with friends 借用了同段 a close alliance with same-sex friends，但\n那是 however 转折之前的铺垫（青春期男性又一次与同性结盟），讲者随即用 however 转\n折强调真正投入的是竞争；取转折前的信息即答错方向。\n借⽤原⽂：the majority of males once again report a close alliance with same-sex friends\nC) 未提及\nPursuing fashion 在整篇录音里没有任何对应，讲者谈的是友谊、竞争、恋爱，从未提及时\n尚，是凭空添加的干扰项。\nD) 未提及\nFulfilling family obligations 同样在录音中无对应，讲者讨论的是同伴友谊与竞争，与家庭\n义务无关，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do children do when they reach late adolescence?", "options": ["A) They prioritize romance over friendship.", "B) They try to strengthen same-sex friendships.", "C) They begin to take friendship more seriously.", "D) They compete intensely for romantic partners."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They prioritize romance over friendship.\n【定位】This is most apt to be the case in late adolescence, when dating and relationships with boys\ntake sharp precedence over sisterhood.\n在青春期后期最容易出现这种情况，那时约会和与男生的关系会明显优先于姐妹情谊。\n【信号】时间标志late adolescence + 优先级信号take precedence over。句中 in late\nadolescence 正是题干同款时间标志，紧跟 take sharp precedence over sisterhood 给出\n明确的优先级判断；时间标志加『谁优先于谁』信号叠加，即为答案句。\n【替换】dating and relationships with boys take sharp precedence over sisterhood ↔\nprioritize romance over friendship\ntake precedence over等于prioritize over\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nstrengthen same-sex friendships 借用了原文反复出现的 same-sex friendships，但本句\n说的是恋爱『压过』姐妹情、即同性友谊被稀释，并非『加强』；方向恰好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：dating dilutes the intensity of same sex friendships\nC) 与原文相反\ntake friendship more seriously 与录音 take sharp precedence over sisterhood 的取向\n正相反——原文是恋爱优先、友谊退居其次，而非更看重友谊。\n借⽤原⽂：dating and relationships with boys take sharp precedence over sisterhood\nD) 原词陷阱\ncompete intensely for romantic partners 把前文讲青春期男性的 competition 一词移花\n接木到『争夺恋爱对象』上；原文从未说他们激烈争夺恋爱伴侣，只说恋爱与友谊之间存在\n竞争，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：there's a competition between romance and friendship", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "Why do disabled job applicants feel reluctant to disclose their disability information?", "options": ["A) They regard it as a matter of sheer privacy.", "B) They are worried about being turned down.", "C) They fear that their condition will be made widely known.", "D) They are afraid to be discriminated against once recruited."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are worried about being turned down.\n【定位】As such, many candidates fear they wouldn't be considered for a position, as a result of\ndisclosing this personal information.\n正因如此，许多求职者害怕：一旦透露这一个人信息，自己就不会被某职位纳入考虑（即\n落选）。\n【信号】fear + as a result 双重信号。many candidates fear they wouldn't be considered for\na position 中的 fear 直接对应题干的 feel reluctant 的心理，as a result of disclosing 又\n点出『因披露而……』的因果，这两个信号合起来标定它就是答案句。\n【替换】fear they wouldn't be considered for a position ↔ worried about being turned down\n怕不被考虑 = 怕被拒\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是怕『不被考虑』，并未说怕病情被广泛知晓；C 借 this personal information\n这个原词诱你联想到『隐私外泄』，但 made widely known（被广泛传开）是录音没说的\n内容，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：as a result of disclosing this personal information\nD) 过度推断\n录音的担忧发生在『还没被考虑、怕落选』阶段；D 说 once recruited（一旦被录用后）被\n歧视，把时间挪到了入职之后，与录音 they wouldn't be considered for a position（怕\n进不去）方向相反，属过度推断兼时间偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：they wouldn't be considered for a position\nA) 未提及\nA 说他们把这当作 a matter of sheer privacy（纯属隐私问题），但录音给的理由是『怕落\n选』这一现实顾虑，并没说他们当作纯隐私来回避，属录音未提及的说法。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "When does the speaker suggest applicants reveal their disability information?", "options": ["A) After receiving a job offer.", "B) During a job interview.", "C) When confirming an interview.", "D) Upon completing a cover letter."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） When confirming an interview.\n【定位】It's most appropriate to discuss a disability when they reply to confirm an interview.\n最合适的（披露）时机，是在他们回复确认面试的时候。\n【信号】most appropriate 推荐句。It's most appropriate to discuss a disability when they\nreply to confirm an interview 用最高级 most appropriate 直接给出『最佳时机』，这种\n带推荐色彩的句子就是『何时』题的答案信号。\n【替换】when they reply to confirm an interview ↔ When confirming an interview\n确认面试时 = confirm an interview\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是『确认面试时(confirm an interview)』而非『面试过程中(during)』；B 借\nconfirm an interview 里的 interview 原词，把时点偷换成 During a job interview，与录\n音差一个动作环节，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：when they reply to confirm an interview\nD) 与原文相反\n录音明确说残疾信息 needn't be put up front in their cover letter or resumé（不必放进\n求职信），D 却让人在 completing a cover letter 时披露，正好取了录音否定掉的那个时\n点，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：This information needn't be put up front in their cover letter or resumé\nA) 未提及\nA 说在 After receiving a job offer（收到录用通知后）披露，但录音推荐的是确认面试阶\n段，全篇没说要等到拿 offer 之后，属录音未提及的时点。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What are people advised to do when filling out their job application form?", "options": ["A) Describe the true state of their health.", "B) Provide all the information required.", "C) Stress the irrelevance of their disability to the job.", "D) Avoid mentioning the name of their disability."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Avoid mentioning the name of their disability.\n【定位】People should avoid sharing the name of their disability or condition.\n人们应当避免说出自己残疾或病症的具体名称。\n【信号】should avoid 告诫句。People should avoid sharing the name of their disability or\ncondition 用 should avoid 直接给出告诫，这种 should/avoid 的建议措辞就是『建议做\n什么』题的答案信号。\n【替换】should avoid sharing the name of their disability or condition ↔\nAvoid mentioning the name of their disability\n避免说出名称 = avoid mentioning the name\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 让人 Describe the true state of their health（如实描述健康状况），录音却劝人 avoid\nsharing the name of their disability or condition（避免说出名称），方向正好相反，是\n与原文相反的干扰。\n借⽤原⽂：People should avoid sharing the name of their disability or condition\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说人们『不应被表格的方式或时机限制』，并未要求 Provide all the information\nrequired（提供所有被要求的信息）；B 借录音反复出现的 information 一词诱你选『信息\n全交』，但全篇恰恰在讲可以有所保留，属原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：people shouldn't feel restricted by this method or timing\nC) 未提及\nC 让人 Stress the irrelevance of their disability to the job（强调残疾与岗位无关）；录\n音填表那段只说『避免说出名称』，并未建议在表格里强调残疾与工作无关，属录音未提及\n的做法。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What did some American researchers find about students' reading comprehension in the presence of background noise?", "options": ["A) Their scores were least affected by music with speech.", "B) It was the poorest when there was background speech.", "C) Their scores were most depressed with instrumental music.", "D) It was disrupted by the sound of an air conditioner or fan."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It was the poorest when there was background speech.\n【定位】Students' scores were most depressed in the presence of background speech. Comprehension\nwas slightly better with the presence of music than with speech.\n在有背景人声（说话声）的情况下，学生的成绩下降得最厉害。有音乐时的理解力比有说话\n声时稍好一些。\n【信号】程度极值 most depressed。答案句靠程度极值信号锁定：scores were most depressed\nin the presence of background speech 中的 most depressed（下降最厉害）直接回应\n“成绩最差出现在哪种声音下”，极值词就是听力比较题的落点。\n【替换】scores were most depressed in the presence of background speech ↔\nIt was the poorest when there was background speech\nmost depressed = poorest\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说有 When the background speech was in a language unfamiliar to participants,\nthere was little if any hindrance，是“陌生语言的人声几乎不干扰”，A 却笼统说成绩受人\n声影响“最小（least affected）”，偷换并反了极值方向，原文是“最差”才对。\n借⽤原⽂：there was little if any hindrance of reading comprehension\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 most depressed 是接 background speech 的，C 却把 most depressed 安到了\ninstrumental music 头上。instrumental music 在录音里反而是 Comprehension was\nslightly better with the presence of music，C 盗用了真实出现过的极值词却配错了对\n象。\n借⽤原⽂：Students' scores were most depressed in the presence of background speech\nD) 原词陷阱\nair conditioner or fan 确在录音中出现，但那是用来定义 General noise is neutral, such\nas that from the sound of an air conditioner or fan（举例说明什么叫一般噪音），并非\n说阅读理解被它“扰乱”，D 借这个原词制造熟耳朵的诱惑，其实答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：General noise is neutral, such as that from the sound of an air conditioner or fan", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn from the British researchers about the degree of interference from background noise?", "options": ["A) It has to do with the type and volume of the background noise.", "B) It has to do with short-term memory for listening comprehension.", "C) It depends on the overlap in processing different kinds of information.", "D) It depends on the participants' ability to concentrate on the task at hand."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It depends on the overlap in processing different kinds of information.\n【定位】It seems the degree of interference from background noise depends on the overlap\nbetween the processing required on the task and the processing required to screen out the\nbackground noise.\n看来，背景噪音造成的干扰程度，取决于“完成任务所需的信息处理”与“屏蔽背景噪音所需的\n信息处理”这两者之间的重叠。\n【信号】因果信号 depends on。答案句靠归因信号锁定：depends on the overlap between the\nprocessing required on the task and the processing required to screen out the\nbackground noise 中的 depends on 直接给出“干扰程度由什么决定”，原因题的落点就在\ndepends on 之后。\n【替换】the overlap between the processing required on the task and the processing required to screen out the\nbackground noise\n↔ the overlap in processing different kinds of information\n两种处理 = 不同信息处理\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nthe type and volume of the background noise 把决定因素说成“噪音的类型和音量”。录\n音确实念了 background noise，但从没说取决于其 type and volume，volume（音量）\n更是录音未提，A 借熟词拼了个原文没有的归因。\n借⽤原⽂：the degree of interference from background noise depends on the overlap\nB) 原词陷阱\nshort-term memory 来自录音 British researchers compared the effects… on short-\nterm memory，那是英国研究的“测试对象/实验任务”，B 把它偷换成“干扰程度取决于短期\n记忆”，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：compared the effects of background speech, vocal music, instrumental music, general\nbackground noise and silence on short-term memory\nD) 未提及\nthe participants' ability to concentrate on the task at hand 听着合理，但英国研究者那\n段从没说干扰程度取决于“专注能力”，这是录音未提的常识性臆测。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is best for students to do when writing essays according to some research?", "options": ["A) Keep everything as quiet as possible.", "B) Play nothing but instrumental music.", "C) Use vocal material as little as possible.", "D) Wear a pair of earphones or headphones."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Keep everything as quiet as possible.\n【定位】When students write essays, however, other research has found it is best to reduce all\nbackground noise as much as possible.\n然而，当学生写作文时，另有研究发现，最好尽可能减少一切背景噪音。\n【信号】转折 however + 建议 it is best to。答案句双信号锁定：转折词 however 提示前面（读\n书可放纯音乐）之外另起一种场景，紧接的 it is best to reduce all background noise as\nmuch as possible 是建议句，转折后的建议正是写作文场景的落点。\n【替换】reduce all background noise as much as possible ↔ Keep everything as quiet as possible.\n减一切噪音 = 保持安静\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nPlay nothing but instrumental music. 里的 instrumental music 来自录音 Instrumental\nmusic will have at worst a slight effect，但那是针对“读书/记忆”场景说的；写作文场景明\n确要求“减少一切噪音”，B 把读书场景的纯音乐建议张冠李戴到写作文上。\n借⽤原⽂：Instrumental music will have at worst a slight effect\nC) 原词陷阱\nUse vocal material as little as possible. 中 vocal 来自英国研究那段 vocal music was\nslightly more disruptive than instrumental，写作文场景要求的是“减少一切噪音”而非单\n单“少用人声”，C 借 vocal 这个原词缩小了范围、答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：vocal music was slightly more disruptive than instrumental\nD) 未提及\nWear a pair of earphones or headphones. 戴耳机听着像隔音的合理办法，但录音从头到\n尾没提 earphones / headphones，属凭常识臆造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "How do people of different personalities react to distractions according to other studies?", "options": ["A) Sociable people were immune to all distractions.", "B) Shy quiet people were most adversely impacted.", "C) Less outgoing people were more affected by silence.", "D) Confident people were unaffected by high-arousal music."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Shy quiet people were most adversely impacted.\n【定位】Everyone performed best in the silent condition, but less sociable people were more\nnegatively affected by each of the distractions.\n所有人在安静条件下表现最好，但不那么善于社交（即较内向）的人，受到每一种干扰的负\n面影响都更大。\n【信号】转折 but 后是真意。答案句靠转折信号锁定：but less sociable people were more\nnegatively affected by each of the distractions 中 but 之后才是关于性格差异的真正结\n论，前半 Everyone performed best in silence 是共性铺垫，转折后才落到“内向者受影响\n更大”。\n【替换】less sociable people were more negatively affected by each of the distractions ↔\nShy quiet people were most adversely impacted.\nless sociable = shy quiet；more affected = most impacted\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nSociable people were immune to all distractions. 中 sociable 来自录音 less sociable，\n但录音说外向者“受影响更小”，并未说“对所有干扰免疫”，且 Everyone performed best in\nthe silent condition 表明人人都受干扰（只有安静最好），A 的 immune to all 是绝对化\n臆断。\n借⽤原⽂：Everyone performed best in the silent condition\nC) 与原文相反\nLess outgoing people were more affected by silence. 里 Less outgoing 对得上内向\n者，但录音说大家在 silent condition 下表现最好，内向者是被“各种干扰”影响更大，而非\n被“安静”影响更大，C 把受影响的对象由 distractions 偷换成 silence，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：less sociable people were more negatively affected by each of the distractions\nD) 原词陷阱\nConfident people were unaffected by high-arousal music. 中 confident、high\narousal music 都在录音里出现（subjected shy quiet people and confident outgoing\nones to high arousal or low arousal background music），但那只是实验设置，录音没\n说自信者“不受高唤醒音乐影响”，D 拼接真实原词造了个原文未下的绝对结论。\n借⽤原⽂：subjected shy quiet people and confident outgoing ones to high arousal or low arousal\nbackground music, general noise or silence", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) estimates\n名词\n【词性槽】主语位置的复数名词\n【依据】估算数量的专用词\nestimates of their population 即 \"对其种群数量的估算\"，与下文 went as high as five\nbillion 给出一个具体估算上限完全对应，rough estimates 也是常见搭配。\n【竞争词】K) excerpts 搭配对象错\nexcerpts 是文章节选，对象为文本，不能修饰 population；且 rough excerpts 在英语里也不\n成搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语位置的复数名词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) vulnerable\n形容词\n【词性槽】were + ___ + to 表语形容词\n【依据】vulnerable to 固定搭配 + 让步逻辑\nbe vulnerable to 是固定搭配 \"易受……影响\"；句首 Despite their huge population 已用\ndespite 标出让步，主句必须给出 \"虽多却脆弱\" 的反差，vulnerable 正合此意。\n【竞争词】E) robust 搭配 + 逻辑双错\nrobust 不与 to 搭配，且语义是 \"强健的\"，与 despite 让步要求的 \"脆弱\" 反转完全相反。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— 把 vulnerable 与 robust 看反。vulnerable 与 robust 都是\n描述生物的形容词，词义相反；只盯 \"形容动物状态\" 而忘了 Despite their huge\npopulation 的让步要求，就会反向填 robust；防坑口诀：despite 之后必反差。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·were + ___ + to 表语形容"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) hatched\n动词\n【词性槽】时间状语从句缺谓语动词\n【依据】鸟蛋孵化的固定搭配\n主语是 the eggs，下一句又紧跟 the parent pigeons would abandon their offspring，\n逻辑链是 \"蛋孵出 → 幼鸟出生 → 父母两周后弃之而去\"，只有 hatched（孵化）能让这条\n链成立。\n【竞争词】J) expired 语义反了\nexpired 表 \"死亡/失效\"，若蛋死了下文就不存在 their offspring 让父母遗弃，前后矛盾。\nN) depleted 搭配错位\ndepleted 主语通常是资源/数量，不能说 \"蛋被耗尽\"；而且后文 the bird population had\nbecome seriously __(34)__ 才是它真正的归宿。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·时间状语从句缺谓语动词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) edible\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 were 后接表语\n【依据】与幼鸽好吃形成并列\n前一句 People discovered that these baby pigeons were really tasty 已说幼鸽好吃，连\n词 also 表 \"成鸟也……\"，下一句又紧跟 came to consider the birds a great __(30)__ 把它\n们视作佳肴，edible（可食用的）与 \"好吃/佳肴\" 一脉相承。\n【竞争词】A) vulnerable 留给 27 题\nvulnerable 在 passenger pigeons were __(27)__ to human intrusion 中与 to 形成\nvulnerable to 固定搭配，是 27 题的标配，这里硬塞会让 27 题无解。\nE) robust 语义跑题\nrobust（强健的）与上下文 \"可吃—佳肴\" 这一主线无关，且后文紧接说人类把它们当美味，与\n\"强健\" 没有逻辑承接。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·系动词 were 后接表语"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) delicacy\n名词\n【词性槽】consider sb/sth + ___ 的宾补名词\n【依据】与 tasty/edible 同色升级\n前两句已铺垫 these baby pigeons were really tasty 和 the adult birds were also quite\n__(29)__ 即可食用，结论自然是把它们视为 a great delicacy（一道美味佳肴）。\n【竞争词】F) refuge 语义脱轨\nrefuge 指 \"避难所\"，与 \"被吃掉\" 的语境完全相反；把动物当 refuge 在逻辑上不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·consider sb/sth + __"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) unprecedented\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 + ___ + 名词作定语\n【依据】前所未有的速度\n下一句 Hundreds of thousands of the birds were being harvested every day 用 \"每天\n数十万只\" 直接量化了这种空前的捕猎规模，an unprecedented pace（以前所未有的速\n度）正与之匹配，且 at an unprecedented pace 是地道搭配。\n【竞争词】H) infinite 程度过头且搭配怪\ninfinite 指 \"无限的\"，pace 一旦无限就不再是速度而成悖论；且 infinite 更适合修饰\nresource，将留给 33 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·冠词 + ___ + 名词作定语"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) tracts\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 + 名词 + of 结构\n【依据】大片土地的专用搭配\ntracts of land 是固定表达，指 \"大片土地\"，与 large __(32)__ of the pigeons' nesting\nterritory were being cleared away for planting crops and creating pasture land 中\n\"被开垦为农田和牧场\" 的语境严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】G) plazas 语义场不符\nplazas 指 \"城镇广场\"，是城市建筑用语，无法描述森林/筑巢地被砍伐开垦的乡野场景。\nK) excerpts 对象错配\nexcerpts 指 \"文章节选\"，对象是文本而非土地，与 nesting territory 不搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 + 名词 + of 结构"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) infinite\n形容词\n【词性槽】an + ___ + 名词的形容词\n【依据】无穷资源呼应数量再多也有尽\n前半句让步 As numerous as the passenger pigeons were 与主句 \"they were not an\n__(33)__ resource\" 形成转折，逻辑应为 \"虽多但并非用之不竭\"，infinite resource（无尽\n资源）正是这个反转点；且为下文 the bird population had become seriously __(34)__\n的锐减做铺垫。\n【竞争词】M) edible 可食用不构成逻辑反转\nedible resource（可食用资源）与前半句 \"数量众多\" 之间不形成 \"虽多但不取之不尽\" 的让步—\n转折逻辑，且 edible 真正用武之地在 29 题描述成鸟。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— 无视让步逻辑只挑元音。33 题 they were not an __(33)__\nresource 中 an 让 M edible 和 H infinite 都满足元音条件，若忽略前半 As numerous as\nthe passenger pigeons were 的让步逻辑，很容易误填 edible；防坑：看到 As…as 让步\n必找反义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·an + ___ + 名词的形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) depleted\n形容词\n【词性槽】had become + 副词 + ___\n【依据】数量被大量耗减\n上文 Hundreds of thousands of the birds were being harvested every day 加上大片栖\n息地被砍伐，必然导致种群锐减；depleted 表 \"被耗尽/锐减的\"，与下文 The last\npassenger pigeons killed in the wild were shot in 1899 的灭绝倒计时呼应。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·had become + 副词 + __"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) vulnerable", "B) unprecedented", "C) tracts", "D) specimen", "E) robust", "F) refuge", "G) plazas", "H) infinite", "I) hatched", "J) expired", "K) excerpts", "L) estimates", "M) edible", "N) depleted", "O) delicacy"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) specimen\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 + 名词作宾语\n【依据】仅存的个体/标本\n同位语 a passenger pigeon named Martha 把空位释义为 \"一只名叫 Martha 的旅鸽\"，\nspecimen 在生物学语境下正好表 \"个体/标本\"，与 \"the last passenger pigeons killed in\nthe wild\" 后所剩唯一一只的画面相称。\n【竞争词】F) refuge 语义指向偏离\nrefuge 指 \"避难所\"，是地点概念，无法被同位语 \"a passenger pigeon named Martha\" 解\n释，且与 shrank to a single 的 \"萎缩到一只\" 这个个体数概念不匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 + 名词作宾语"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Employers attach more importance to applicants' computer skills than their language competence.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】雇主更看重计算机技能而非语言能力\n【定位】According to the National Association of Colleges and Employers' 2016 report, study abroad\nand foreign language fluency were not very influential in the employee hiring processes, but\n55 percent of employers looked for computer skills on applicants' resumes.\n根据美国全国大学与雇主协会 2016 年的报告，留学经历和外语流利度在招聘过程中影响并\n不大，但 55% 的雇主会在求职者简历上寻找计算机技能。\n【改写】Employers attach more importance to applicants' computer skills ↔\n55 percent of employers looked for computer skills on applicants' resumes\n“更看重” = “55% 雇主主动寻找”量化体现\nthan their language competence ↔\nstudy abroad and foreign language fluency were not very influential in the employee hiring\nprocesses\n比较结构的“次要一极”用否定量化呈现\n【辨邻】 D D 段虽然写了 computer science jobs have helped boost wages in the U.S.，但讲\n的是宏观岗位与工资，没有触及“雇主招聘时更看重计算机技能 vs 语言能力”这一对\n照，只是话题相邻。\ncomputer-related jobs hold the top seven positions in STEM fields for highest number of workers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "One U.S. state senator proposed that high school students be allowed to study either foreign language or computer coding.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】美国一位州参议员的二选一提案\n【定位】\"I think the opportunity to give people a choice is important,\" says Florida state\nSenator Jeremy Ring, who introduced a bill last year that would allow Florida students\nto choose between foreign language and coding classes for the purpose of university\nadmissions requirements.\n佛罗里达州参议员 Jeremy Ring 说：“我认为给人们选择的机会很重要。”他去年提出一项法\n案，将允许佛州学生为大学入学要求在外语和编程课之间二选一。\n【改写】One U.S. state senator proposed ↔\nFlorida state Senator Jeremy Ring, who introduced a bill last year\n通名→专名 + “proposed” = “introduced a bill”\nhigh school students be allowed to study either foreign language or computer coding ↔\nallow Florida students to choose between foreign language and coding classes\n“either A or B” = “choose between A and B”\n【辨邻】 F F 段虽然也提 Ring，但聚焦 The Florida bill died this year after passing 35:5 in\nthe state Senate 这一下游事件，不再陈述“提出 either-or 提案”这一动作本身。\nan identical bill will be reintroduced within the next year by others on his behalf", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Learning languages broadens students' international perspective and nurtures mutual respect among peoples, according to a high school language teacher.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高中语言教师谈国际视野与相互尊重\n【定位】\"Our world is shrinking but its problems are really growing,\" says ACTFL National Language\nTeacher of the Year Ted Zarrow, who teaches high school Latin in Westwood, Massachusetts,\nand has also studied Spanish, French, German, Italian and Greek. \"We need to find a way to\nput ourselves at the global table and to treat each other with mutual respect. And learning\nlanguages allows us to do that because language is not part of culture, language is culture.\"\nACTFL 年度全国语言教师 Ted Zarrow（在麻州 Westwood 教高中拉丁语，同时学过西、\n法、德、意、希腊语）说：“我们的世界在缩小，但问题在膨胀。我们需要找到办法让自己\n坐到全球的桌前并彼此尊重。学语言能让我们做到这一点，因为语言不是文化的一部分，语\n言就是文化。”\n【改写】Learning languages broadens students' international perspective ↔\nlearning languages allows us to do that\n“拓宽国际视野”= “让我们坐到全球桌前”\nnurtures mutual respect among peoples ↔ treat each other with mutual respect\n“mutual respect”原词复现\naccording to a high school language teacher ↔\nteaches high school Latin in Westwood, Massachusetts\n通名身份对应专名身份\n【辨邻】 N N 段同样提到 Zarrow 与“respect”，但讨论的是 The study of language fosters a\nrespect for diversity, a respect for ethnicity and really a respect for language 与\n外语应更早开始，不是题干所指的“国际视野 + 民族间相互尊重”立场。\nThe study of language fosters a respect for diversity, a respect for ethnicity and really a respect\nfor language", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "One U.S. state will see to it that programming classes are taught by quality teachers.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】某州保障编程课由高质量教师任教\n【定位】Instead, the state will provide support for higher quality certified teachers for\nprogramming classes.\n相反，肯塔基州将为编程课提供更高水平的认证教师作为支持。\n【改写】One U.S. state will see to it ↔ the state will provide support\n“see to it” = “provide support / 出面保障”\nprogramming classes are taught by quality teachers ↔\nhigher quality certified teachers for programming classes\n“质量教师 + 编程课”名词搭配的主被动改写\n【辨邻】 C C 段 a lack of skills training for teachers 讲的是当前缺乏师资培训这一现状，并未\n承诺由谁出面去保障“quality teachers for programming classes”。\na lack of skills training for teachers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Statistics show while computer-related jobs have been on the rise, foreign languages have become less appealing to American students since mid-1990s.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】1990 年代中期以来外语吸引力下降的统计\n【定位】According to the 2016 U.S. News/Raytheon STEM Index, computer science jobs have helped\nboost wages in the U.S., and computer-related jobs hold the top seven positions in STEM\nfields for highest number of workers. Foreign language interest, on the other hand, is\ndeclining for the first time since 1995.\n根据 2016 年 U.S. News/Raytheon STEM 指数，计算机科学岗位推高了美国工资，且计算\n机相关岗位在 STEM 领域中占据了从业人数最多的前七位。另一方面，外语兴趣自 1995 年\n以来首次下降。\n【改写】computer-related jobs have been on the rise ↔\ncomputer science jobs have helped boost wages in the U.S., and computer-related jobs hold the\ntop seven positions in STEM fields\n“岗位上升” = “拉高工资 + 占据高就业前七位”\nforeign languages have become less appealing to American students since mid-1990s ↔\nForeign language interest, on the other hand, is declining for the first time since 1995\n“less appealing” = “interest is declining”+ “mid-1990s” = “1995”\n【辨邻】 L L 段虽然同样涉及“computer skills”与“foreign language”对照，但时间锚是\nNational Association of Colleges and Employers' 2016 report 和 by 2024，与陈\n述句的“mid-1990s”时间锚不符。\nNational Association of Colleges and Employers' 2016 report", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "All school subjects are said to be essential to students' well-rounded development.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】所有学科都对学生全面发展不可或缺\n【定位】Exposure to a wide breadth of subjects and material results in well-rounded students who are\nable to make informed decisions … about what they want to pursue.\n广泛接触各类学科与素材会培养出全面发展的学生，他们能就自己想追求的方向做出明智的\n决定。\n【改写】All school subjects are said to be essential ↔\nWe teach algebra because it's important and we should teach foreign language and coding for\nthe same reason\n由具体到全称的概括上位\nstudents' well-rounded development ↔\nExposure to a wide breadth of subjects and material results in well-rounded students\n“well-rounded”原词复现\n【辨邻】 O O 段 Coding is an incredibly important 21st century skill 与 learning a foreign\nlanguage 强调“两者皆重要”，并未提出“所有学科都对全面发展不可或缺”这一全称命\n题，与题干的“All school subjects”范围不符。\nthe two skill sets are essential but unrelated", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "There is consensus among most educators that coding should be taught in schools but should not replace foreign language.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】教育者就“加编程但不取代外语”的共识\n【定位】When it comes to allowing coding to fill foreign language requirements, though, most\neducators agree: Coding should be added to curriculums, but not at the expense of foreign\nlanguage classes.\n不过，谈到让编程顶替外语要求时，大多数教育者达成共识：编程应被加入课程，但不能以\n牺牲外语课为代价。\n【改写】consensus among most educators ↔ most educators agree\n动词→名词的名词化改写\ncoding should be taught in schools but should not replace foreign language ↔\nCoding should be added to curriculums, but not at the expense of foreign language classes\n“not at the expense of” = “not replace”\n【辨邻】 O O 段虽然立场相近 computer science should be negotiated into curriculums\nrather than replacing foreign language outright，但表达者是 foreign language\nand coding experts agree，与题干“most educators”的身份锚不符。\nforeign language and coding experts agree that computer science should be negotiated into\ncurriculums rather than replacing foreign language outright", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "One study showed that foreign language learning improved students' academic performance.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】一项研究表明外语学习提升学业表现\n【定位】A 2007 study showed that foreign language students outperformed their non-foreign language\npeers on standardized tests after only two to three years of study.\n2007 年的一项研究表明，外语学生仅在两到三年的学习后，就在标准化考试中胜过非外语\n学生。\n【改写】One study showed ↔ A 2007 study showed\n通称研究→具体年份研究\nforeign language learning improved students' academic performance ↔\nforeign language students outperformed their non-foreign language peers on standardized\ntests after only two to three years of study\n“提升学业表现” = “在标准化考试中胜出”\n【辨邻】 L L 段的 National Association of Colleges and Employers' 2016 report 讨论的是招\n聘中雇主对计算机技能的偏好，并非外语学习对学业表现的影响。\nstudy abroad and foreign language fluency were not very influential in the employee\nhiring processes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Being short of funding and qualified teachers, schools lag behind the fast developing computer science field.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学校因缺钱缺合格教师而落后于计算机科学\n【定位】The computer science field is growing faster than schools can keep up because of budget\nconstraints and a lack of skills training for teachers.\n由于预算紧张和教师技能培训不足，计算机科学领域的发展速度超过了学校所能跟上的\n速度。\n【改写】Being short of funding and qualified teachers ↔\nbudget constraints and a lack of skills training for teachers\n“缺资金 + 缺合格教师”同义替换\nschools lag behind the fast developing computer science field ↔\nThe computer science field is growing faster than schools can keep up\n“落后于” = “跟不上”的句式重构\n【辨邻】 G G 段 higher quality certified teachers for programming classes 讲的是某州出面保\n障教师，对应 39 题，而不是 44 题“因缺资金缺教师而落后”的现状陈述。\nthe state will provide support for higher quality certified teachers for programming classes", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "A distinguished high school language teacher also believes it is advisable to start learning a foreign language at an earlier age.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一位知名高中语言教师认同外语应更早学\n【定位】Zarrow agrees that foreign language education should begin earlier, but says it is possible to\nwork both computer programming and foreign language learning into schools evenly.\nZarrow 同意外语教育应更早开始，但表示可以把编程与外语学习都均衡地纳入学校课程。\n【改写】A distinguished high school language teacher ↔\nZarrow agrees that foreign language education should begin earlier\n“知名高中语言教师” = Zarrow（ACTFL 年度教师）\nalso believes it is advisable to start learning a foreign language at an earlier age ↔\nZarrow agrees that foreign language education should begin earlier\n“also believes” = “agrees” + “earlier age” = “begin earlier”\n【辨邻】 M M 段 I just think it's something you have to start early and not just have\nsomething that you do for a couple of years in high school 是 Ring 先提出“早学”\n立场，但 Ring 是参议员而非“high school language teacher”，且与题干 also 的“附\n议者”关系不符。\nI just think it's something you have to start early\nK K 段虽然也是 Zarrow 出场，但谈的是 put ourselves at the global table and to\ntreat each other with mutual respect，并未表达“外语教育应更早开始”这一立场。\nlearning languages allows us to do that because language is not part of culture, language is culture\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n引入背景：编程作为高需求技能正进入 K-12 课程，部分州考虑允许学生用编程课替\n代外语课，但教育者反对。\n信息指纹 用编程替代外语的提议起点\nB\n教育界对小学是否教编程意见不一，但在“能否用编程顶替外语”这一点上多数教育者\n达成共识：可以加，但不能挤掉外语。\n信息指纹 教育者共识——加编程但不挤外语\nC\n支持者认为编程也是一门语言，且是 21 世纪的语言；但学校受预算与师资不足限\n制，跟不上计算机科学的发展。\n信息指纹 预算与师资不足拖慢学校\nD 数据对照：计算机相关岗位拉高了美国工资且占 STEM 高雇佣前七位；而外语兴趣自\n1995 年以来首次下滑，2009-2013 高校语言注册人数减少超 11 万。\n信息指纹 STEM 指数与 1995 年来外语首降\nE 佛州参议员 Jeremy Ring 提案：允许高中生在外语和编程之间二选一用于满足大学入\n学要求。\n信息指纹 佛州参议员的二选一提案\nF 该佛州法案在参议院 35:5 通过后因整体立法机构未行动而搁浅；Ring 表示明年会有\n人替他重新提出。\n信息指纹 佛州法案搁浅与再提\nG 肯塔基州类似法案因教育者反对被修改为只推动计算机科学教育、不涉及外语要求，\n转而支持为编程课提供更高水平的认证教师；华盛顿州法案则允许两年编程课替代两\n年外语。\n信息指纹 肯塔基州转向提供高质量认证教师\nH\n德州 2013 年通过法案，允许学生在外语课表现不佳后才能用编程替代；Tynker 联合\n创始人 Mandyam 反对“因学不好就免修”，类比代数、化学，主张广泛学科有助于学\n生全面发展。\n信息指纹 Mandyam 以代数化学类比，强调全面发展\nI\n在 28 个州和华盛顿特区，计算机科学课已可顶替数学或科学的高中毕业要求（2013\n年仅 12 州）；支持者要求改算外语，反对者强调编程与外语技能并重。\n信息指纹 28 州顶替数学/科学的现状\nJ\n研究显示双语与认知发展、智力、记忆、解决问题能力相关；2007 年研究表明外语学\n生在标准化考试中两三年后即优于非外语学生；2014 年研究指出编程时使用的是大脑\n的语言区，但批评者仍警惕单一语言在国际经济中的劣势。\n信息指纹 2007 年研究：外语学生在标准化考试中胜出\nK 麻州 Westwood 高中拉丁语教师、ACTFL 年度语言教师 Zarrow 表示：世界在变小但\n问题在变多，需要把自己摆到全球桌前并相互尊重，学语言能做到这一点，因为语言\n就是文化。\n信息指纹 Zarrow：语言促全球相互尊重\nL 招聘方更看重计算机技能：2016 年报告显示留学与外语流利对招聘影响不大，55%\n雇主在简历上找计算机技能；但劳工局预测 2024 年程序员需求将下降 8%。\n信息指纹 55% 雇主看重计算机技能\nM Ring 承认外语技能重要，但怀疑学区没有时间在校内同时把编程和外语都纳入课程；\n他认为语言学习必须从小开始，而非高中里读两年。\n信息指纹 Ring 怀疑时间不够同时纳入两者\nN Zarrow 同意外语教育应更早开始，并相信编程与外语可以在校内并重，建议沉浸式双\n语项目（半天英文半天外语）。\n信息指纹 Zarrow 也主张外语更早起步\nO 结论段：外语和编程专家都认为计算机科学应被协商纳入课程而非直接替代外语；\nMandyam 称两种技能都重要但不可替代，教育者必须想办法同时教好两者。\n信息指纹 协商纳入而非取代——总结性立场", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say is the issue all major economic powers have to address?", "options": ["A) How to ensure the sustainable growth of their tech giants.", "B) How to keep the competitiveness of their tech companies.", "C) How to break up the powerful giant tech companies.", "D) How to stop tech companies from gaining monopoly."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say is the issue all major economic powers have to address?\n直接问\"是什么 issue\"，是对作者陈述的具体内容的提取。\n【拆句】主干是 What does the author say is the issue，all major economic powers have to\naddress 是省略 that 的定语从句修饰 the issue；have to address 是\"必须解决\"，不是\"可\n以解决\"，程度词不能弱化。\n【定位】But there's one issue on which all these countries see eye to eye: Technology companies are\ntoo big, too powerful, and too profitable. And that power is only likely to intensify, leaving\ngovernments with no choice but to confront it head-on by taking the companies to court,\npassing new competition laws, and perhaps even breaking up the tech giants.\n但有一个议题，所有这些国家看法一致：科技公司过于庞大、过于强大、利润过高。而这种\n权力只会加剧，使各国政府别无选择，只能正面迎击——把公司告上法庭、出台新的竞争\n法，甚至拆分这些科技巨头。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nensure the sustainable growth 是\"确保巨头持续增长\"，与原文\"政府必须正面对抗、\n甚至拆分\"完全相反。\nB) 偷换概念\ncompetitiveness of their tech companies 是\"维护本国科技公司的竞争力\"，原文谈的\n是\"限制巨头权力\"，竞争力≠垄断权力，主语对象与目标都被偷换。\nC) 以偏概全\nbreak up the powerful giant tech companies 仅是原文列举对抗手段中的一项\nperhaps even breaking up the tech giants，且原文用 perhaps even (甚至) 表示这\n只是极端可选项，不是\"必须解决的 issue\"本身。\nD) ✓ 正确\nTechnology companies are too big, too powerful, and too profitable ↔\ntech companies from gaining monopoly\nconfront it head-on by taking the companies to court, passing new competition laws, and\nperhaps even breaking up the tech giants\n↔ How to stop tech companies\n预判\"必须阻止科技公司权力进一步膨胀/取得垄断地位\"与 How to stop tech\ncompanies from gaining monopoly 在主谓宾三层 (政府-stop-巨头取得垄断) 上一一\n对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the suspension of Ant Group Co.'s stock offering suggest?", "options": ["A) All attempts to evade regulation are doomed to failure.", "B) All attempts to monopolize sales must be cracked down.", "C) All companies must be regulated by the government.", "D) All companies, domestic or foreign, are created equal."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】What does the suspension of Ant Group Co.'s stock offering suggest? 含 suggest，\n问\"该事件暗示什么\"，是要求基于事实做一步逻辑延伸。\n【拆句】主干 What does the suspension suggest；of Ant Group Co.'s stock offering 是介词短\n语修饰 suspension。注意 stock offering 是\"股票发行\"，整个主语是\"蚂蚁集团股票发行被\n暂停\"这一事件本身。\n【定位】The draft rules followed the surprise suspension of a $37 billion stock offering by\nbillionaire Jack Ma's Ant Group Co., making clear that no company can evade the\ngovernment's regulation.\n新规草案紧随蚂蚁集团 370 亿美元股票发行被突然叫停之后出台，明确表示任何公司都不能\n逃避政府监管。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nAll attempts to evade regulation are doomed to failure 把原文\"不能逃避\"延伸为\"所\n有逃避企图必然失败\"，多走了\"企图必败\"这一步，且原文没说\"企图\"这一主体。\nB) 偷换概念\nAll attempts to monopolize sales must be cracked down 把对象从\"逃避监管\"偷换\n为\"垄断销售\"；原文从未谈\"销售垄断\"，且 Ant Group 的 IPO 暂停与 monopolize\nsales 也无直接因果。\nC) ✓ 正确\nno company can evade the government's regulation ↔\nAll companies must be regulated by the government\nthe government's regulation ↔ regulated by the government\n从预判\"任何公司都逃不过监管\"出发，只有 All companies must be regulated by\nthe government 完整且对等地承接\"no…evade → all…must be regulated\"这一逻辑\n变换。\nD) 无中生有\nAll companies, domestic or foreign, are created equal 谈\"公司是否生而平等\"，是宪\n法/法律观念，原文从未涉及；且 domestic or foreign (内外资) 这一区分原文也没有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "How are smaller companies impacted by tech giants' business expansion?", "options": ["A) They can no longer do business independent of tech giants.", "B) They are frequently denied access to tech giants' platforms.", "C) They have to change marketing strategies to keep customers.", "D) They no longer have the power to price their own products."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How are smaller companies impacted by tech giants' business expansion? 直接问\"小\n公司怎样被影响\"，要的是原文给出的具体影响，不需要做推断。\n【拆句】主干 How are smaller companies impacted，by 引导施动者 tech giants' business\nexpansion；被动语态的施动者就是影响来源，找原文时要找\"巨头扩张→小公司怎么了\"的\n因果链。\n【定位】Tech giants are increasingly assuming powerful positions in banking, finance, advertising,\nretail, and other markets that force smaller businesses to rely on their platforms to\nreach customers.\n科技巨头正越来越多地在银行、金融、广告、零售等其他市场占据强势地位，迫使小企业必\n须依赖它们的平台才能触达客户。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nforce smaller businesses to rely on their platforms to reach customers ↔\nThey can no longer do business independent of tech giants\nto reach customers ↔ do business\n预判\"小企业被迫依附巨头平台\"与 They can no longer do business independent of\ntech giants 是同一命题的肯定式与否定式表达，二者一一对应。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey are frequently denied access to tech giants' platforms 说小企业\"经常被拒绝\n接入巨头平台\"，与原文\"被迫依赖平台\"恰好相反——原文的痛点是\"必须用\"，不是\"不\n让用\"。\nC) 无中生有\nchange marketing strategies to keep customers 谈\"改变营销策略以留住客户\"，原\n文未谈营销策略，也未谈\"留住老客户\"；原文谈的是\"通过谁去触达客户\"，主体动作完全\n不同。\nD) 偷换概念\nno longer have the power to price their own products 把\"被迫依附\"偷换成\"无定价\n权\"。原文第四段确实提了 the old focus on pricing power no longer applies，但那\n是说\"反垄断不再以定价权为衡量\"，并非\"小企业失去定价权\"，主体与对象都被偷换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What have EU countries done to confront the power of digital giants?", "options": ["A) They have imposed strict regulation over digital giants' advertising.", "B) They have considered regulatory action to promote fair competition.", "C) They have limited sales of digital giants' products.", "D) They have sought to protect consumers' privacy."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What have EU countries done 直接问\"做了什么\"，是对原文动作的提取，无推断信号。\n【拆句】主干 What have EU countries done，不定式 to confront the power of digital giants\n表目的——回答须是\"为了对抗巨头\"所采取的动作，不是其他动机的动作。\n【定位】Led by Margrethe Vestager, the European Union's competition chief, countries have sought to\npolice the market and encourage fair play.\n在欧盟竞争事务专员 Margrethe Vestager 的带领下，各国致力于监管市场、推动公平\n竞争。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nimposed strict regulation over digital giants' advertising 把监管对象限缩为\"广\n告\"。原文 advertising revenue 只是\"巨头吸收个人数据并由此攫取广告收入\"问题的一\n部分，并非欧盟监管的对象本身；选项把局部问题升格为全部监管对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\npolice the market ↔ considered regulatory action\nencourage fair play ↔ promote fair competition\n选项 considered regulatory action to promote fair competition 把\"police the\nmarket + encourage fair play\"打包成\"regulatory action + promote fair\ncompetition\"，与预判完全对应。\nC) 无中生有\nlimited sales of digital giants' products 谈\"限制巨头产品销售\"，原文未提及任何关于\n销售/产品限制的措施。\nD) 以偏概全\nsought to protect consumers' privacy 是\"保护消费者隐私\"，原文 vacuuming up\npersonal data 确实涉及隐私，但那是问题描述而非欧盟主要动作；本段对动作的总结\n是 police the market and encourage fair play 这一更上位的表达，选项以\"隐私保\n护\"代替\"市场监管+公平竞争\"是以偏概全。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What do Americans generally think of social media companies according to the author?", "options": ["A) They are invading people's privacy.", "B) They are increasingly influential.", "C) They are becoming untrustworthy.", "D) They are growing out of control."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度题）\n【判型】What do Americans generally think of social media companies 问\"普遍怎么看\"，要的\n是评价/态度极性，是态度题。\n【拆句】generally 是程度副词\"普遍/总体上\"，提示答案要覆盖大多数人的态度，不能拿少数派或个\n别极端表态做答；according to the author 限制信息源只能是文章本身。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\ninvading people's privacy 谈\"侵犯隐私\"，原文第七段未直接谈\"美国公众认为社交媒体\n侵犯隐私\"，前文 privacy, control over data 是\"反垄断专家应考虑的因素\"清单，不是\n美国公众对社媒公司的看法。\nB) 答非所问\nincreasingly influential (越来越有影响力) 是中性偏正的极性，与原文负向极性矛盾；\n同时原文第七段未直接评价\"影响力大小\"，而是评价\"影响是好是坏\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe public in general has grown increasingly skeptical of social media companies ↔\nThey are becoming untrustworthy\nMore than 60% say the sector has a negative effect on the country ↔\nbecoming untrustworthy\n选项 C 在极性 (负向)、强度 (becoming 渐进体) 上都与原文 increasingly skeptical 完\n全对偶，且不引入任何原文没有的额外维度。\nD) 过度推断\ngrowing out of control 是\"日益失控\"——\"失控\"的判断超出原文。原文只说公众觉得\n社媒\"负面影响大、需要更多监管\"，并未说\"已经失控\"；要求更多监管恰恰说明\"还可被\n监管\"，与\"out of control\"相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the popular assumption about international tourism?", "options": ["A) Its benefits may compensate for the adverse environmental consequences.", "B) Its rapid development is attributed to people's improved living standard.", "C) It appeals to people in places with favorable geographical conditions.", "D) It contributes to the economy of destination countries and regions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the popular assumption about international tourism 直接问\"那个普遍看\n法是什么\"，要求复述原文里被点名的那一句假设，不要求推断。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is the popular assumption about international tourism，What is 引导\n特殊疑问，about international tourism 是后置介词短语修饰 assumption，结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Travel is often portrayed as a tool for personal growth and tourism as an economic motor for\ndestination countries and cities. There is a tendency to assume that tourism is good for\neveryone involved.\n旅行常被描绘为个人成长的工具，旅游业则被描绘为目的地国家和城市的经济发动机；人们\n倾向于假设旅游业对所有相关方都是好事。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项 A 谈\"收益可以弥补不利环境后果\"，但 P1 只笼统说\"旅游对各方都好\"，从未提\n出\"补偿环境代价\"的对比框架。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 B 说\"旅游业的快速发展归因于生活水平提高\"，原文虽提到 Born from the\naccessibility of mass air travel——\"源于大众航空旅行的可及性\"——但归因对象是\"航\n空可及\"而非\"生活水平\"。\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 C 说旅游业\"吸引地理条件优越地区的人\"，但原文 has been popularized as\n\"holiday-making\" in regions that offer comparative advantages of sand, sun and\nsea 讲的是旅游业\"在\"那些地区被普及，并不是这些地区的人\"被\"旅游业吸引。\nD) ✓ 正确\ntourism as an economic motor for destination countries and cities ↔\nIt contributes to the economy of destination countries and regions\n预判中\"推动目的地经济\"在选项 contributes to the economy of destination\ncountries and regions 中找到对应，且 destination 一词原词重合，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn from some studies about uncontrolled tourism development?", "options": ["A) It gives rise to an increase in mass confrontations.", "B) It incurs local residents' antagonism to tourists.", "C) It inhibits the steady growth of local economy.", "D) It brings in a large chunk of mobile population."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from some studies about uncontrolled tourism development\n要求复述研究的结论，结论句必为原文明写，不需推断。\n【拆句】题干主干 What do we learn，from some studies 是状语，about uncontrolled tourism\ndevelopment 修饰 studies 限定研究主题，句子无从句、无否定，理解上无陷阱。\n【定位】Uncontrolled tourism development has become a primary driver of social and environmental\ndisruption. Tourism studies provide much documentation of the many negative social impacts\nof tourism and resulting resentment that local populations direct toward visitors.\n失控的旅游发展已成为社会和环境破坏的首要推手。旅游研究大量记录了旅游业的种种负面\n社会影响，以及本地居民由此对游客产生的怨恨。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n选项 A 说\"导致大众对抗增加\"，原文虽提到 Recent protests in ports of call like\nVenice and Barcelona，但只是举例两地抗议，且这是 uncontrolled tourism\ndevelopment 的背景，不是\"研究告诉我们的内容\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nresulting resentment that local populations direct toward visitors ↔\nIt incurs local residents' antagonism to tourists\n预判中\"研究记录居民对游客的怨恨\"在选项 It incurs local residents' antagonism to\ntourists 中找到几乎一一对应的同义替换，锁定 B。\nC) 无中生有\n选项 C 说\"它抑制本地经济稳定增长\"，原文仅说收益分配不均（no guarantee that\nmultinational hotel chains will allocate these benefits equitably），并未说旅游会\n让本地经济\"不增长\"。\nD) 答非所问\n选项 D 说\"它带来大量流动人口\"，原文虽提到 over 1.2 billion tourists across\ninternational borders annually，但这是\"旅游业体量\"的描述，不是\"失控发展\"研究的\n结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Why does the author say local residents of popular destinations often feel frustrated?", "options": ["A) They fall victim to social conflicts and environmental disturbances.", "B) They have little opportunity to enjoy themselves on cruise ships.", "C) They cannot find employment in multinational hotel chains.", "D) They do not think they benefit as much as they deserve."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why does the author say local residents of popular destinations often feel\nfrustrated 用 Why does the author say，是请考生复述作者给出的原因，原文必有明示\n原因。\n【拆句】Why does the author say 引导原因疑问，宾语从句 local residents... feel frustrated 完\n整，主语后置定语 of popular destinations 限定 residents，副词 often 提示常态，无否\n定无让步，无陷阱。\n【定位】Residents often become frustrated when the benefits of tourism are not felt locally. Although\nit can generate foreign exchange, income and employment, there's no guarantee that\nmultinational hotel chains will allocate these benefits equitably among local communities.\n当旅游业的好处在本地感受不到时，居民们常常感到失望。尽管旅游业可以带来外汇、收入\n和就业，但跨国酒店集团并不一定会把这些好处公平地分配给本地社区。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 A 说\"他们沦为社会冲突和环境干扰的受害者\"，原文 P3 谈过失控的社会环境破\n坏，但 P5 解释 frustrated 的原因是经济收益分配不公，而不是\"自己成了社会/环境的\n受害者\"。\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 B 说\"他们没机会在邮轮上玩乐\"，但原文 when people stay at large resorts or\non cruise ships 谈的是\"游客\"在邮轮消费，而不是\"本地居民\"没机会享乐。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 C 说\"他们在跨国酒店连锁找不到工作\"，原文 Although it can generate foreign\nexchange, income and employment 明确说旅游业\"能\"带来就业，问题在于\"分配不\n公\"而非\"无工作\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe benefits of tourism are not felt locally ↔\nThey do not think they benefit as much as they deserve\n预判中\"没拿到应得份额\"在 They do not think they benefit as much as they deserve\n落地，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How does the author say local residents in destination communities respond to tourism activity?", "options": ["A) They endeavor to adapt to it.", "B) They readily adopt new lifestyles.", "C) They immerse tourists in their culture.", "D) They try to upgrade their business models."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How does the author say local residents in destination communities respond to\ntourism activity 是 How does the author say...的格式，等同于 Why does the author\nsay，复述作者所写的反应方式。\n【拆句】How does the author say 主问句，宾语从句中 local residents in destination\ncommunities 是主语，respond to tourism activity 是谓宾结构；in destination\ncommunities 是后置介词修饰 residents，结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】In recent decades, local residents in destination communities also have found themselves\nnegotiating new cultural boundaries, class dynamics, service industry roles and\nlifestyle transformations.\n近几十年来，目的地社区的本地居民发现自己正在为新的文化边界、阶层动态、服务业角色\n和生活方式转变进行磋商和调适。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nnegotiating new cultural boundaries, class dynamics, service industry roles and lifestyle\ntransformations\n↔ They endeavor to adapt to it\n预判\"居民在主动调适\"在 They endeavor to adapt to it 落地：endeavor 抓住主动努\n力的语气，adapt 抓住调适的方向，锁定 A。\nB) 过度推断\n选项 B 说\"他们乐于采纳新生活方式\"，但原文 as local residents adopt the behaviors\nof tourists 与 tourism activity corresponds to increased social problems 并列，说\n明这种采纳被作者视为社会问题，绝非\"乐于\"。\nC) 答非所问\n选项 C 说\"他们让游客沉浸在本地文化里\"，方向完全相反；原文是 being prepared to\nadapt to local customs and norms, rather than expecting local conditions to\nadapt to travelers，恰是\"游客适应当地\"，但本题问的是本地居民的反应，不是游客的\n反应。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 D 说\"他们尝试升级商业模式\"，原文虽提到 local entrepreneurs whose\nbusinesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic benefits，但这是\n作者对游客的建议\"应该奖励哪类商家\"，与居民是否\"升级商业模式\"无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can tourists do to exert more positive impacts on the tourist destinations?", "options": ["A) Show interest in local customs and lifestyles.", "B) Seek possibilities to invest in local companies.", "C) Use the services provided by local businesses.", "D) Give favorable comments about their services."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What can tourists do to exert more positive impacts on the tourist destinations\n在问可采取的行动，原文最后两段是显式建议段，属于明示信息复述。\n【拆句】What can tourists do 主干，to exert more positive impacts 是不定式状语表目的，on\nthe tourist destinations 介词短语修饰 impacts；more 是 positive 的比较程度修饰，提\n示\"更多\"而非\"唯一\"，无陷阱。\n【定位】Informed travelers also are better able to distinguish between multinational companies and\nlocal entrepreneurs whose businesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic\nbenefits for local residents. Such businesses are in love with the destination and are therefore\ndeserving of market reward.\n见多识广的游客也更能区分跨国公司与本地企业家——后者的生意能为本地居民带来直接的\n社会、环境与经济效益。这类企业对目的地怀有情感，因而值得获得市场回报。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n选项 A 说\"对当地风俗和生活方式表示兴趣\"，原文 being prepared to adapt to local\ncustoms and norms 谈的是\"准备好去适应\"，且该建议是\"第一条\"，而题干问的是\"对\n目的地施加更多正面影响\"，需的是更具体的对策——本地消费。\nB) 过度推断\n选项 B 说\"寻找投资本地公司的可能性\"，原文谈的是 deserving of market reward 即\n消费层面的市场奖励，从未要求游客投资本地企业。\nC) ✓ 正确\nlocal entrepreneurs whose businesses provide direct social, environmental, and economic\nbenefits for local residents\n↔ Use the services provided by local businesses\ntourism is a market-based activity and works best when consumers reward better performers ↔\nUse the services provided by local businesses\n预判中两个要素\"消费\"与\"本地企业\"在 Use the services provided by local businesses\n一一落地，锁定 C。\nD) 无中生有\n选项 D 说\"对其服务给好评\"，原文 deserving of market reward 的市场奖励是消费行\n为，没有任何关于点评/留言的字样。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
